/* Wau-Bun — Part 1, the same scenes retold in a tauter, more
   immediate voice: the events, people, places and turns of the 1856 text,
   in present-day English, with nothing added. Original prose written for
   this app — nothing is quoted from any other book.
   Generated — do not hand-edit; the passages live in wau-bun/dark/<scene>.txt.
   Loaded only when a reader opens this mode. */
var WAUBUN_DARK_PART1 = {
  s1: ["We went aboard the steamer Henry Clay on a dark, rainy evening in September of 1830, bound for Green Bay. Everyone we knew in Detroit had told us how fortunate we were. The alternative was passage in one of the little schooners that were, at that time, the ordinary means of reaching the few scattered settlements on Lakes Huron and Michigan.", "They all had a story ready, their own or a friend's. Days held fast on the St. Clair flats with nothing to do but wait out the water. Furious head-winds off Thunder Bay. Calms at Mackinac, calms at the Manitous, and no way of knowing how long any of them would last. The account that made us feel luckiest of all was true, and it belonged to our own family: a relative had left Detroit in the month of June and did not set foot in Chicago until the September following. Three months. A sail-vessel has been known to do the same run in four days.", "None of it would have deterred me. I was going to see that country at last, and not merely see it — live in it. For years it had been a region of romance to me. When I was a child my greatest pleasure had been the letters of a dear relative describing his home and his mode of life in the Indian country; later there was his happy account of the tour he made with General Cass, in 1820, to the sources of the Mississippi. Earlier than either, there was a long word I had spelled out at my teacher's knee, Mich-i-li-mack-i-nac, syllable by syllable. Vast lakes. Boundless prairies. Forests with no end to them. Now I was to see it. It was to be my home.", "We laughed the whole way to the quay. The streets of Detroit were unpaved and deep in mud, and a cart was the only vehicle that could get through them, so a cart carried us along the dark by-ways down to the water. We laughed again going below — the stair to the little room they called the Ladies' Cabin was narrow and near enough to upright to be called a ladder. The accommodations delighted us. Measured against a schooner they were the very height of comfort and convenience, and better still, the cabin held nobody but myself, one other lady, and two little girls.", "For the first twenty-four hours nothing could have been pleasanter. Some of the passengers were officers, old friends of ours. We had brought plenty of books, and now and then one of the gentlemen read aloud from them, and broke off to admire the country going past — the untouched woods, the enormous reach of water with not one sail anywhere upon it, all of it magnificent and all of it empty — and then turned to a cigar, or to a game of euchre, to get through the hours."],
  s2: ["The crossing of Thunder Bay was lovely for a while. The bay did not intend to let its name go unearned on our account. The storm arrived all at once and came on hard, and worse, it did not pass. We took the pitching and rolling of that little boat as we ought to, and the closeness below, and the sea-sickness. All of that we had known was coming and had made up our minds to. What we had not counted on was a fresh kind of misery, and it nearly finished off our philosophy for us.", "The rain came down in sheets and found every seam and every pore in the deck and the moulding overhead. It ran down the stair-way. It worked through the joints and the cracks. It soaked the carpet first and then the bedding, and at last, by stress of weather, we were driven right out of our own quarters and into the Gentlemen's Cabin. The gentlemen gave way to us very handsomely and did everything they could for our comfort, and we had just begun to congratulate ourselves on having found a harbor in the middle of our trouble when the seams over that cabin opened too, and the water came down on our heads in such quantity that umbrellas were no use at all. Nothing remained for the ladies and the children but the berths, which in that room had stayed dry, and there we lay the whole day long. Our dinner was handed to us on our pillows. The men picked out the driest patches of floor, put up their umbrellas, and sat under them telling us funny stories and saying cheerful things until the rain finally stopped, and at nine that evening word came down that we had made the pier at Mackinac."],
  s3: ["Mr. and Mrs. Robert Stuart took us in with the warmest affection, in a great hospitable house where we had been looked for several days already.", "What such a shelter is worth can only be understood by people who reach one as we reached that one, tossed about and soaked through. There was a fire burning bright and hot, and there were faces turned toward us with nothing in them but the kindest concern, and between them every trace of the day's weariness and vexation went out of me.", "We talked a good while, pleasantly, and then the servants were called in. The chapter of God's word was read out solemnly; the hymn sung; the prayer of praise and thanksgiving offered; and then we were shown to our beds.", "I do not mean to draw those noble friends here, on the night I first met them. The life of Robert Stuart deserves a better pen than mine to write it. Anyone who has known him, and still more anyone who has stayed under that hospitable roof, will carry the impression of the man to their last hour — the bearing of him, the genial humor, the benevolence that never tired, the upright and uncompromising way he held to a principle, the ardent philanthropy, the noble disinterestedness. Irving, in his \"Astoria,\" and Franchere, in his \"Narrative,\" set down a good deal of his early character, along with passages of his history that are thrilling and romantic to read; but the most valuable part is in neither of them, which is what he became afterward, a Christian gentleman.", "Of his beloved partner, who is living still, and mourning her way through a bereaved and solitary pilgrimage, cheered by the memory of her long, useful course as a \"Mother in Israel,\" we will say nothing more than to offer her the incense of loving hearts, and our prayers for the best blessings her Father in heaven can send."],
  s4: ["Michilimackinac — the gem of the Lakes. How bright it looked, and how beautiful, when we walked out the next morning. The rain was gone, and it had left the whole world glittering, with the sun coming up out of the water of Lake Huron far off to the east. The bay lay in front of us, still not quite settled after the storm, and already there were canoes on it, and the fishermen's boats putting out their nets for the trout and the whitefish that are the treasure of that deep. Along the beach stood the wigwams of the Ottawas, who had come over to the island to trade. Their people came out of the lodges to have a look at us. Then somebody knew Shaw-nee-aw-kee, and a shout of welcome went up — he had lived among them seven years, and there was not one of them he was a stranger to.", "A hand taken, and an emphatic \"Bon-jour - bon-jour\": that is the customary greeting between an Indian and a white man.", "\"Do the Indians speak French?\" I asked of my husband.", "\"No, this is a fashion they have learned of the French traders during many years of intercourse.\"", "The Canadian engagés were every bit as warm. Each of them came trotting up in turn to pay his respects to \"Monsieur John\" and to deliver a long stream of felicitations in a patois I could make nothing whatever of. I had to take the good wishes rained down upon \"Madame John\" entirely on faith. All I could catch out of any of it was the hope that I would be happy and contented in my \"vie sauvage.\""],
  s5: ["What we had risen early to see was the Mission-house and the school beside it, planted here a few years before by the Presbyterian Board of Missions. Mr. and Mrs. Stuart cared about that place more than about anything else on the island, and they had reason to be pleased with it that morning: it was thriving, and it seemed to promise a great deal of usefulness in the years ahead. They had lived here a long while. They had seen what the island used to be — worldly, dissipated, given over to itself — and they had seen it turned around under God's blessing on Christian effort until you could nearly say of it that \"Religion was every man's business.\" The mission was the beloved child of the handful of Protestant families gathered about it, and the thing they all had in common. Mr. and Mrs. Ferry ran it with zeal and good sense, the congregation kept it warm with encouragement, and its name stood high. It was pleasant to watch what teaching and religion did to the children: how they took the mischief and the trickiness out of the half-breed, and how they stirred the genuine Indian out of his stolid apathy.", "Those were the palmy days of Mackinac. The island held the head-quarters of the American Fur Company, and it was the entrepôt for the whole Northwest. Everything moving in either direction — supplies and trade goods going out, furs and the products of the Indian country coming back — passed through the parent house here or through one of its many outposts, strung along Lake Superior and Lake Michigan, down the Mississippi, and off into country farther away than that.", "Most people know that from the time the old French possessions passed to the British Crown, every Indian tribe but the Miamis and the Wyandots held firmly to the British side. The independence won by the United States changed nothing in that. Our Government could neither charm their friendship nor buy it. Great Britain, it must be said, bid high to keep them. Year after year the leading men of the Chippewas, the Ottawas, the Pottowattamies, the Menomonees, the Winnebagoes, the Sauks and Foxes, and of tribes living farther off still, made the long journey from their own country to Fort Malden in Upper Canada to collect the yearly presents of their Great Father over the water. Keeping them on the payroll was a masterly piece of policy, and in the last war between the two countries it let the men who practised it do fearful execution with such allies at their back.", "What was handed out was worth having: blankets, broadcloths and strouding, calicoes, guns, kettles, traps, silver-work of every sort — arm-bands, bracelets, brooches, ear-bobs — looking-glasses, combs, and a hundred other trinkets besides, and nobody counted them out with a mean hand.", "To the magazines and store-houses of the Fur Company here at Mackinac came all the upper tribes, to sell what they had and to buy what they wanted, the same goods named above and ammunition as well. Ammunition their British friends very commendably neglected to furnish, along with money and liquor.", "They brought furs first, of many kinds, and some of them worth a great deal — beaver and otter, marten, mink, silver-gray fox and red, wolf, bear, wild-cat, musk-rat, and smoked deer-skins. They brought maple-sugar in quantity, and a good deal of Indian corn and petit-blé,[1] and beans, and the folles avoines,[2] which is wild rice. The squaws put in their own share of the merchandise: moccasins, hunting-pouches, mococks — little birch-bark boxes worked over with porcupine-quills and packed with maple-sugar — matting of a neat and lasting weave, and small toy models of Indian cradles, of snow-shoes, of canoes, and of anything else you please.", "In those years it was nothing out of the ordinary to look out and count a hundred canoes or more coming in on the island at one time, every one of them loaded with goods for trade. Set beside that the squadrons of big Mackinac boats arriving without pause from the outposts, carrying the furs and peltries and buffalo-robes that the distant traders had gathered up, and you begin to see the size of what the American Fur Company was doing here, and how many human lives, near and far off, hung on some thread of it.", "It is no wonder that a philanthropic mind, looking out over all these races of uncultivated heathen, should reach ahead in imagination to the day when the white man, spending his energies without rest and sacrificing self and fortune without stint, should see his red brethren climb the scale of social civilization — when Education and Christianity would walk together and make \"the wilderness blossom as the rose.\"", "Not one of those good souls, glad that morning over what their work at Mackinac had accomplished, guessed what was coming. In under twenty-five years there would be nothing left of all these numerous tribes but a few scattered bands, squalid, degraded, hardly a trace remaining of the lofty character they once had. Their lands would be talked out of them or torn out of them. The graves of their fathers would be turned up by the ploughshare. They themselves would be pushed off toward the setting sun, farther with each removal, until the earth itself was literally begrudged them as a place to rest on.", "We could not stay long at the school. The Henry Clay was to sail at two o'clock, and before that we meant to see something of the village and the country around it and then to dine with an old friend of my husband's, Mr. Mitchell. Walking at our ease along the white gravel of the road, I had the houses of the old inhabitants pointed out to me one after another. One was the home of Madame Laframboise, an Ottawa woman. Her husband had taught her to read and write, and from that time on she had used what he gave her for the good of the young people of her own nation. Every day she took a class of them into her house and taught them those branches, and with them the principles of the Roman Catholic religion, which she held to with her whole heart. She had a great deal of energy and enterprise in her, and she was tall, commanding, and dignified in the way she carried herself. Her husband had been killed at his post, off in the trading country, by a Winnebago named White Ox. After that she went out to the posts herself, kept the clerks and engagés under her eye, and made certain with her own judgement that the business was being run in a regular and profitable way.", "The Agency-house stood at the foot of the hill the fort was built on, and it had a piazza and gardens, luxuries you did not expect to find here. It was a lovely spot, for all that everything under cultivation in this cold northern latitude comes up stunted and dwarfish.", "Near it stood the buildings the Fur Company's officials used, a rickety and primitive-looking collection that did no great credit to the architectural skill of my husband. He had overseen the building of them, he told me, when he was hardly more than a boy.", "Then there were the houses of the Dousmans, the Abbotts, the Biddles, the Drews, and the Lashleys, running away along the base of that beautiful hill, and above them all the white walls and buildings of the fort. The climb up is so steep that on the sheer face of it nearest the beach they have built staircases to get from the bottom to the top."],
  s6: ["My head hurt hard that morning — the boat's motion of the day before, still working in me long after I had stepped off it. I did not want to give in to it. So I let myself be walked past every part of the little settlement that could be shown in the short time we had, and then we went to Mr. Mitchell's.", "Mrs. M. received us: very pretty, delicate, French on one side and Sioux on the other, and raised at Prairie du Chien on the Mississippi. At Fort Crawford she had been a great belle among the young officers there — so much so that when the post-surgeon killed himself, people laid it to an attachment he had formed for her and she had not returned. What held me was not that story. It was the softness of her manners, and the way her voice rose and fell as though it were half sung, a music I came to know afterward as the mark of every woman in whom French and native blood ran together.", "Another woman was of the company, a lady then staying at the Mission. She would have me lie down on the sofa, and tended my aching head with great kindness. She sat beside me and talked of the new sphere opening in front of me, and then she asked:", "\"Do you not realize very strongly the entire deprivation of religious privileges you will be obliged to suffer in your distant home?\"", "\"The deprivation,\" I said, \"will Surely be great, but not entire, for I shall have my Prayer-Book, and, though destitute of a church, we need not be without a mode of worship.\"", "In the years after, out in the lonely wilderness and comforted by that precious book, I thought back on that conversation more times than I can count, and blessed God that so long as I kept it I could never be without \"religious privileges.\"", "We had not yet left the dinner-table when the little steamer's bell went, calling us aboard. There was no time for a proper leave-taking. We said what we could to all those kind friends, and carried away with us their hearty wishes for a safe and prosperous voyage.", "There is hardly a finer thing to look at than Mackinac from the water. The shore let go of us and the whole island arranged itself in one view: the beach sloping down, wigwams scattered along it, canoes drawn up here and there, the odd irregular houses, the white walls of the fort above them, and higher still a farther eminence carrying the remains of old Fort Holmes. Seen whole and complete, it made the outline that had given the place its first name — Mich-i-li-mack-i-nac, the Big Turtle.", "And the water under us. Living water, so clear that the fish showed plainly in it, gliding and darting and turning back; drop a thing over the side and you can follow it down fifty or sixty feet to the bottom, a dollar lying on the green floor of the lake and looking no bigger than a half dime. I have never been able to think the enthusiastic lady foolish who cried, \"Oh! I could wish to be drowned in these pure, beautiful waters!\"", "Off the extreme western point of the island my husband turned me toward the northwest and showed me a promontory a long way out. Point St. Ignace, he said, and told me what it was: one of the earliest white settlements anywhere on this continent. The Jesuit missionaries had a church and a school standing there in 1607, the same year that white men settled St. Augustine in Florida, and one year before anyone laid out Jamestown in Virginia.", "Of everything those devoted men built, nothing is standing now. What is left of them is the memory of the work, and in most cases nothing but the names of the places where they spent their love on their savage brethren. One building survives: the little French church at Sandwich, across from Detroit, the single witness left of the zeal and self-sacrifice of those pioneers of Christianity.", "We passed \"Old Mackinac\" on the main land, which makes the southern edge of the straits, and then the shore opened and let us out onto the broad water of Lake Michigan. Everyone who travels here knows what was done at that old fort, and so does everyone who has read our history: how in Pontiac's day a game of ball was got up outside the walls, friendly enough to watch, so that the officers and the soldiers came out to see it played; how the ball was tossed, very neatly, over the wall; how the players poured in after it as though that were all they were after, and took possession, and massacred the garrison.", "Far to the south the little Indian village of L'Arbre Croche caught the last of the setting sun and glittered. Nothing else on that whole enormous wooded coast gave any sign that a person lived there. Gigantic forest-trees came down to the edge, and here and there a small glade of prairie opened out to the water — ground any farmer would have looked at and wanted, fat with promise — and not one footprint of a man anywhere upon it. We left it to its solitude and laid our course westward across the waters."],
  s7: ["The weather that belongs to the equinox found us again before we had made the mouth of Green Bay — rain, and wind coming in gusts — and it stayed with us until the night we came onto the flats, some three miles short of the settlement. That was where the little steamer stuck, \"fast and hard.\" A week in those quarters had been enough for nearly everyone; the storm looked better than another hour cooped up inside. So we put ourselves into the little boat and went, wind and rain and blackness and all, and in due time we came ashore."],
  s8: ["We could not have picked a worse week to come into Green Bay. A treaty was being made between the United States Government and the Menomonees and Waubanakees, so the place held not only the commissioners and their clerks and their officials, but traders and claimants and travelers and more idlers than anyone could count. Nearly all of them had packed themselves into the one hotel the settlement could offer, a house of fair size standing near the river. We came in off the open boat cold and dripping and walked into the long dining-room, and the sight of what was in it entertained us enormously. People sat in knots wherever there were chairs. Others, newly arrived like ourselves, simply stood, because the seats had run out long before the guests did. The landlord sat through all of it with his hands in his pockets, calm, indifferent, phlegmatic in the way of a Pennsylvania Dutchman.", "His wife made up for him. She was fat and capable and never still, trotting from one end of the room to the other, breaking off to scold somebody who, burn his skin, had not done what he was told, and breaking off again to laugh at some joke thrown her way until her whole frame shook with it.", "Her welcome to us was warm enough. Her answer was not. We asked, \"Can you accommodate us?\" and she said, \"Not I. I have got twice as many people now as I know what to do with. I have had to turn my own family out of their quarters, what with the commissioners and the lot of folks that has come in upon us.\"", "\"What are we to do, then? It is too late and stormy to go up to Shanty-town to seek for lodgings.\"", "\"Well, sit you down and take your supper, and we will see what we can do.\"", "And she did. She turned up a little nook for us somewhere in that house, and we took it gladly, well away from all those people.", "What separated us from the next room was a thin board partition, and the next room belonged to General Root, of New York, one of the commissioners of the treaty. Our steamer had carried the mail up with us — in those days a rare blessing for settlements that far out — and the General's share of it reached him around bedtime. Every dispatch had to be opened and read before he would consider going to bed. His eyes were weak, so his secretaries did the reading aloud. He was also somewhat deaf, and kept telling the man to raise his voice, and so, through that flimsy division, we heard the contents of the letters and the General's opinions of them whether we wished to or not.", "\"What is that, Walter? Read that over again.\"", "We coughed. We hemmed. We knocked over several pieces of furniture. Nothing reached them; they were too far into their business to notice anything happening in the world outside it. Had we been politicians, every secret of the working-men's party was ours that night, and there was capital in it for anyone who wanted to make some."],
  s9: ["Morning, and rain again. Rain, and rain, and nothing in the world but rain. It made no difference to the gentlemen. They meant to take a small boat out to the steamer and bring the luggage up whatever the weather, and among that luggage — the part of it no one could afford to lose — were several boxes of silver, the annuity money for the Winnebagoes at the Portage.", "I went out onto the piazza with some of the others to watch them go. Somebody pointed across the water at Fort Howard, set on a jutting point of the far shore about three-quarters of a mile off: the old barracks, the picketed inclosure, the walls, all of it odd to my eye, and looking ancient and venerable although in fact it had been thrown up only lately. Then we all turned and found the boat again. It had the middle of the river by then. One of the passengers was standing upright in the stern, giving directions, as far as we could tell.", "\"That is rather a venturesome fellow,\" said one, \"if he isn't careful he will lose his balance.\" The words were barely out before the man went over backward — a clean somersault — and was gone into the water.", "\"Oh!\" cried I, \"he will be drowned!\"", "The gentlemen laughed. \"No, there he is, they are helping him in again.\"", "The boat came about at once and brought the party back to shore. I watched them land, and it was not until one of them stepped out and came toward me streaming wet and laughing that I understood I was looking at my own peculiar property. He chose to treat the whole business as a joke. I thought it a rather sad beginning to our Western experience.", "He let himself be talked into leaving his effects in the care of his friends. He changed his clothes and was settling in to stay quietly with me when a vehicle drew up at the door, and out of it came a face we both knew and were glad of — our old friend, Judge Doty.", "Word of our arrival had reached him, and he had come to carry us off at once to his hospitable mansion. We wanted nothing better. We gathered up our bags and our traveling-baskets and went with him without any further ceremony.", "The drive took us first along the edge of Navarino and then through Shanty-town — of the two names, only the second has been honestly earned — through mud and mire, over roads that were barely roads, up and down pitches steep enough to break a neck, until we came to our friends' little brick house. Mrs. Doty took us in with a kindness that was plain sisterly truth, and there was so much welcome in the place that we were at home in it almost at once.", "They had been expecting us, and the invitations were already written out. The whole circle of Green Bay society was to be assembled to meet us at an evening party. In a new country that is simply the established way of doing honor to a guest or a stranger.", "We asked after Captain Harney, who had kindly offered to bring a boat and a crew of soldiers down from Fort Winnebago to carry us up to that place, our destined home. He had not yet arrived. That left us free, and we made our arrangements for a few days of social enjoyment at \"the Bay.\"", "People shut away as these were from the world at large were glad of everything we had brought with us: the news, the particulars of how life was being lived and how people were behaving, the thousand small items no newspaper of that day dreamed of furnishing, the fashions, and the general gossip that a lady is erroneously thought to be more au fait of than a gentleman.", "I remember that day chiefly as one nearly unbroken stream of talk, given and taken, with hardly a pause anywhere in it. Every one of the party except me had already made the trip — the voyage, rather — up the Fox River and down the Wisconsin to the Mississippi.", "The anecdotes came mostly out of one journey in particular, made by the three of them in company with a French trader and his two sisters, who were then making their debut as Western travelers. There was Mademoiselle Julie, who would help herself, without asking anybody's leave, to a fine damask napkin or two for wiping out the ducks before they went to the fire. There was the impossibility of persuading either sister that the table apparatus ought to be washed and rinsed nicely before it was packed away in the mess-basket, so that at every meal another good napkin had to be whisked out to wipe the dishes with. And there was the young gentleman's answer to it all, which was to hunt his misappropriated articles out of every nook and corner of the boat, tie them up with a cord, and hang them over the stern to make their own way down the Wisconsin to Prairie du Chien.", "Best of the lot was the story of M. Rolette himself. Somewhere on the route — crossing Winnebago Lake, I believe it was — the travelers fell in with one of the Company's boats headed down to Green Bay for supplies. M. Rolette was one of the Company's agents and the men in that boat were his employés, and he had been some weeks away from home. To meet like that on those lonely waters and trade the news was an event, and both parties felt it as one.", "The boats were stopped. Greetings, in earnest. Then the questions, each one crowding the last.", "\" Eh bien - have they finished the new house?\"", "\" Oui, Monsieur.\"", "\" Et la cheminée, fume-t-elle? \" (Does the chimney smoke?)", "\" Non, Monsieur.\"", "\"And the harvest - how is that?\"", "\"Very fine, indeed.\"", "\"Is the mill at work?\"", "\"Yes, plenty of water.\"", "\"How is Whip?\" (his favorite horse.)", "\"Oh! Whip is first-rate.\"", "Store, farm, the business in every branch of it — the whole of it gone through and settled to his satisfaction, with nothing left to ask. No reason to hold the boats any longer. Time to be off.", "\" Eh bien - adieu! bon voyage! \"", "\" Arrachez, mes gens! \" (Go ahead, men!)", "Then suddenly - \" Arrêtez! arrêtez! \" (Stop, stop!)", "\" Comment se portent Madame Rolette et les enfans? \" (How are Mrs. Rolette and the children?)", "The day wore itself out at last, excitement and all, and we went to our rest — thankful above everything else that General Root and his secretary were not lying close by our bed's head with their budget of political news."],
  s10: ["Sleep took me, but it did not keep me. At the very first hint of dawn something came up to me through the floor, out of the room beneath ours — a chant, low and level, one figure of melody turned over and over, lifting and then sinking again into a kind of grieving. It did not sound to me like anything a living person would make. Wild. Strange. Past all accounting for. I woke my husband in real fear, and he told me, quite calmly, that what I was listening to was the Indians greeting the opening day.", "Some Menomonees had been given shelter for the night in the kitchen below us, a kindness offered and a kindness taken; and having kept their custom, which never varies, of singing their morning hymn, they let the sound die away and lay down again to sleep. Their listener overhead did not. I could find nothing in it but beauty — a song of praise raised at daybreak by the untaught sons of the forest. Set that beside the civilized and Christianized world, where so many lie down at night and rise in the morning without one breath of thanksgiving to the Almighty Preserver who keeps them, without so much as a thought of Him who gives His angels charge concerning them. The lesson of that hour has never once faded from me. I have liked to believe that in some of those voices the devotion ran as pure as it runs in the Christian who says the sainted Ken's words, \"Awake, my soul! and with the sun,\" etc.", "Callers came to welcome me to the West. Among them were Mr. and Miss Cadle, who stood then at the very first steps of the work that afterward flourished — the education of Indian and half-breed children. Nothing had been built yet. No school-houses, no chapel. We walked over the ground they had chosen for them, and I listened, and was moved by it, while they laid out everything that was to come: the good that would be done, the success that would crown their efforts, the heathen tamed and brought to their Saviour and to the blessings of civilized life. What followed has shown how little the zeal of a few can do when the cupidity of the many stands against it."],
  s11: ["The evening party was much what an evening party is anywhere else. What held me was the one thing I had never had before: the conversation of several young ladies I was presented to, all of them born at Green Bay or close by it. Their mother was a Menomonee; their father a Frenchman whose people had settled the country generations back, and who is thought to come off the same branch of the Grignon family that the daughter of Madame de Sévigné married into. The family is said to keep a quantity of old papers and records that would leave you with that impression, though the spelling of the name has drifted a little since. However that may be, the Miss Grignons were dignified and well-bred to a striking degree, and there was something in their soft voices and their unstudied, original way of putting a thing that a stranger found very attractive.", "They also opened a door I would rather have left shut. I said how impatient I was to see my new home, and they looked at me in plain astonishment. \"Vous n'avez donc pas peur des serpens?\"", "\"Snakes! was it possible there were snakes at Fort Winnebago?\"", "\"At the Portage! oh! yes - one can never walk out for them - rattle-snakes - copper-heads - all sorts!\"", "I am not a timid woman by nature. Still, I will admit that from that evening the serpens sonnettes and the siffleurs were not a subject I could be perfectly indifferent about.", "One of those young ladies was tall and moved beautifully, with a rich, blooming color and a dark eye that caught the light, and she would have been noticed in any drawing-room in the country. Another had a gentle sweetness and a cultivated taste that made everyone who knew her regret it when she went, later, into the seclusion of a convent.", "Captain Harney and his boat came in on time, and then the real preparations began, with Mrs. Doty overseeing every comfort of the trip with the greatest kindness. The mess-basket was packed with every good thing there was — ham and tongue, biscuit and plum-cake — besides the plain provisions, crackers and bread and boiled pork, the pork being an article a lady was assumed to be far too fastidious to touch, though she were starving in the woods.", "We had taken on three Canadian voyageurs to have charge of the tent, the mess-basket, and matters and things in general. They were to cut the wood for our fires, get our meals, and lend a hand to whatever needed doing. A messenger had gone ahead as well, up to the Kakalin, or rapids, twenty-one miles above us, to tell Wish-tay-yun — the most accomplished guide there is through the difficult passes of that river — to hold himself ready for us on a named day."],
  s12: ["Before we left there was room in the calendar for one more party, and this one was to be a \"real Western hop.\" Nobody who went to that dance at Mrs. Baird's ever forgot it. Everybody on Green Bay came — that was the way people said it then, on the Bay, not at it — young and old alike, the whole settlement turned out. The young officers came up from Fort Howard and looked very smart in their uniforms. Finery that had lain folded away for years was brought out again, because every one of us meant to do the strangers honor by appearing at his absolute best. It was agreed beforehand that nothing given there had ever equalled it. The house was taken over from end to end. Mr. B.'s office made one wing, and the desks and the seats in it were carried out without any ceremony at all to give the dancers more floor. The front rooms were put in order, and so was the kitchen, since a primitive sort of visitor did now and then appear who would rather sit quietly back there and smoke his cigar than stand about with the company. In those days that was an emergency you had to be ready for.", "There was no holding down the mirth of that room. Nothing restrained anybody but good manners; nobody was weighed down with conventionalities. It was the plain, warm, hearty enjoyment that pleasant hospitable people can raise between them when there is just enough of the French element in the house to put an edge on any amusement. In a country that new, where what counted at a gathering was the number of the company rather than the quality of it, the circle could not honestly be called a select one.", "I knew that going in. So when a clumsy little man with a broad red laughing face came waddling across the floor to the chair I had dropped into after a dance, I was more amused than surprised by what he said.", "\" Miss K - -, nobody hain't never introduced you to me, but I've seen you a good many times, and I know your husband very well, so I thought I might just as well come and speak to you - my name is A - -.\"", "\"Ah! Mr. A - -, good-evening. I hope you are enjoying yourself. How is your sister?\"", "\"Oh! she is a great deal worse - her cold has got into her eye, and it is all shot up.\"", "Then he turned bodily on a lady[4] seated near me, young, beautiful, quick, sparkling with wit and with humor that was entirely her own.", "\"Oh! Mrs. Beall,\" he began, \"what a beautiful gown you have got on, and how handsome you do look! I declare you're the prettiest woman in the room, and dance the handsomest.\"", "\"Indeed, Mr. A - -,\" replied she, putting down her love of fun and arranging her face into demureness, \"I am afraid you flatter me.\"", "\"No, I don't - I'm in earnest. I've just come to ask you to dance.\"", "That is what it costs to be too charming."],
  s13: ["The plan had Judge Doty in the boat with us as far as the Butte des Morts, where an attendant of his would be standing by with horses to take him along to Mineral Point. He had a court to hold there.", "We left his pleasant house on a bright and beautiful morning to begin the passage up the Fox River. Captain Harney meant to stay on at \"the Bay\" a few days yet, but he came by to walk us down and see us installed in whatever comforts the boat had to offer.", "The boat lay down at the river-bank, and everything between us and it was ploughed ground or worse, so he gave me his arm and talked as we went about Mrs. Twiggs, the commanding officer's wife. Fort Winnebago had held no other white lady since the spring, and she was waiting on the arrival of a companion and a friend with no patience left at all. We had met in New York not long after her marriage, so we would not be strangers to one another. I had the whole of it settled in my mind already. Two of our sex instead of one, my piano brought up safe, the Post Library we had bought unpacked at last, everything arranged and in its place, and then, far off in the wilderness as we were, we would make the happiest little circle anyone could imagine. Nothing I looked forward to in that country was anything but bright.", "The boat waiting for us was a Mackinac boat of moderate size, some thirty feet of dark-looking hull, crewed by soldiers with our own three voyageurs added to them. Amidships stood a frame of light posts holding up a canvas roof, and canvas curtains hung from it that could be dropped along the sides and the ends, the way they are on a country stage-coach, or rolled up again to let in light and air.", "Into the middle of this little cabin, or saloon, went the box that held my piano, and onto the box went a mattress, to serve as a divan through the day and as a place to sleep at night if the weather ever turned too wet or too disagreeable for camping. Beside it were stowed the boxes of silver: the annuities my husband would count out to his red children, owed them by the stipulation of the treaty. The mess-basket sat within easy reach. We had bought, besides, two large square covered baskets from the Waubanakees, the New York Indians, to hold our outer clothing and bedding, and those did double duty as very convenient little work-tables and dinner-tables.", "Being a true daughter of New England, I need hardly say that embroidery and knitting-work came along with me. Pencils and books needed no saying at all.", "The greater part of our furniture, and all the housekeeping goods we had laid in at New York and Detroit, were to come up behind us in another boat, in the charge of men whose professed trade was the bringing of cargo safely up the rapids as far as Fort Winnebago. It is an undertaking of some three weeks and an immense amount of labor, and the owner of the goods may think himself a lucky man to receive them at the end of it at all, in whatever wet, broken and dilapidated state they reach him. That was why the things we valued most travelled with us, and why we were willing to be a little crowded for the sake of it.", "Until that morning I had never in my life seen a gentleman in a shirt of any color at all. A white collar and bosom, spotless, belonged among the \"notions\" which \"Boston,\" and New England \"folks\" after it, entertained on the subject of a gentleman's toilette. Mrs. Cass had warned me, laughing, that a traveling equipment wanted calico shirts every bit as much as it wanted patch-work pillow-cases; and sure enough, some tidy little Frenchwoman had been at our divan-pillows with taste and skill both, and turned every one of them out in garb of the brightest and most variegated kind.", "The Judge and my husband went gay in the deepest blue and the deepest pink. Each had by him besides a cap of bright red, the voyageurs' bonnet rouge, or tuque, and in deference to the lady aboard it was to come out only when a nap in the afternoon became unavoidable — after a hearty dinner, or a dull book, or too long a stretch without exercise.", "The Judge was an admirable man to travel with. He had spent years in that country; he had gone with General Cass on the expedition that reached the head-waters of the Mississippi; and about the early times, the customs and the people he carried a vast fund of anecdote.", "Two of his accounts of the way justice used to be administered in those days have stayed with me.", "At the Bay there had been an old Frenchman named Réaume, excessively ignorant and grasping with it, though otherwise tolerably good-natured. This was the man they made justice of the peace. Two men came before him once, the one complaining, the other defending himself. He heard the complaint out with patience, and he heard the defense out, and then he stood up with great dignity and delivered himself of a judgment.", "\"You are both wrong. You, Bois-vert,\" to the plaintiff, \"you bring me one load of hay, and you, Crély,\" to the defendant, \"you bring me one load of wood, and now the matter is settled.\" So far as anyone ever heard, no exception was taken to that verdict.", "The one story brought on another, and the second was set on the Mississippi, at Prairie du Chien.", "A Frenchman there, another justice of the peace, went by the name of \"Old Boilvin\" with everybody. His office stood just beyond the fort walls, and the officers made quite a fashion of lounging in there in the morning, to get some sport out of an idle hour and to drink with the old gentleman a glass of brandy-and-water, which he called \"taking a little quelque-chose.\"", "A soldier named Fry stood accused of stealing a calf belonging to M. Rolette and killing it, and the constable — a bricklayer by the name of Bell — had been sent to arrest him and bring him in for trial.", "The gentlemen were paying their customary morning visit to the justice when a noise started up in the entry, and then somebody knocked.", "\"Come in,\" cried Old Boilvin, getting up and crossing to the door himself.", "Bell. - \"Here, sir, I have brought Fry to you, as you ordered.\"", "Justice. - \"Fry, you great rascal! What for you kill M. Rolette's calf?\"", "Fry. - \"I didn't kill M. Rolette's calf.\"", "Justice, shaking his fist. - \"You lie, you great - - rascal! Bell, take him to jail. Come, gentlemen, come, let us take a leetle quelque-chose.\"", "The Canadian boatmen sing the whole time they are rowing or paddling, and nothing heartens them like having the \"bourgeois\" lead off the singing himself. Should the passengers join in on the refrain, and the ladies above all, the compliment counts for more still.", "Their songs run light and cheerful. Most of them carry some small satire or witticism in them, built to bring on a spirited chorus, and now and then an uproarious one.", "Verse and refrain go somewhat like this:", "BOURGEOIS. - Par-derrière chez ma tante, Par-derrière chez ma tante.", "CHORUS. - Par-derrière chez ma tante, Par-derrière chez ma tante.", "BOURGEOIS. - Il y a un coq qui chante, Des pommes, des poires, des raves, des choux, Des figues nouvelles, des raisins doux.", "CHORUS. - Des pommes, des poires, des raves, des choux, Des figues nouvelles, des raisins doux.", "BOURGEOIS. - Il y a un coq qui chante, Il y a un coq qui chante.", "CHORUS. - Il y a un coq qui chante, etc.", "BOURGEOIS. - Demande une femme à prendre, Des pommes, des poires, des raves, des choux, etc.", "CHORUS. - Des pommes, dos poires, etc.", "BOURGEOIS. - Demande une femme à prendre, Demande une femme à, etc.", "It runs on in that fashion, the counsel handed down one piece at a time — \"Ne prenez pas une noire, Car elles aiment trop à boire,\" \"Ne prenez pas une rousse, Car elles sont trop jalouses.\"", "And when every last qualification has been named and objected to, drawn out beyond all reckoning by the endless repetition of the chorus, the bourgeois lifts his voice and calls out - \"Whoop la! à terre, à terre - pour la pipe!\"", "The voyageurs stop every five or six miles without fail, to rest and to smoke, and that used to be the measure of distance in the country — not so many miles, but so many pipes.", "Some of the Canadian melodies are very beautiful, and I can imagine no more exhilarating way of travelling than over those waters, in the midst of all that wild magnificence, with the measured stroke of the oars keeping time to \"Le Rosier Blanc,\" \"En roulant ma Boule,\" or \"Lève ton pied, ma jolie Bergère.\"", "The height of the fun, though, was a comic piece which they sang over and over and which somehow never went stale. It went about like this:", "BOURGEOIS. - Michaud est monté dans un prunier, Pour treiller des prunes.", "La branche a cassé - CHORUS. - Michaud a tombé?", "BOURGEOIS. - Ou est-ce qu'il est?", "CHORUS. - Il est en bas.", "BOURGEOIS. - Oh! reveille, reveille, reveille, Oh! reveille, Michaud est en haut! Etiquette required that everyone look astonished at Michaud's luck in keeping his place in the tree after the branch had broken under him, and then the joke had to be worked through every variety of fruit-tree that Michaud might be supposed capable of climbing."],
  s14: ["The first day's travel put us at the Kakalin by evening, where another branch of the Grignon family had their place. They made us very welcome. I was so impatient to begin my life in the forest that I would sooner have had the tent set up on the river bank and done with the comforts of civilization then and there — but to say so would have been a slight, and perhaps worse than a slight, so we behaved better than that. We sat down to hot venison steaks and crêpes and a cup of that coffee no one on earth makes like a Frenchwoman, which after a whole day out of doors is the most reviving thing there is.", "The Kakalin is a rapid in the Fox River, and a serious enough one that the heavy lading has to come out of a boat and go round by land, while the boat itself is poled up, or hauled up on cords, against the water. It is only one of a string of rapids and chûtes, or falls, that lie between that spot and Lake Winnebago, twenty miles further on.", "We breakfasted the next morning, took leave of our hosts, and made ready to go on. The bourgeois had been up since an early hour standing over his men while they worked the boat and the cargo past the Kakalin. The rains had left the paths through the woods and along the bank soft and miserable for walking, so I was loaded into an ox-cart instead, and jolted along a road that had never been leveled, meeting every stump and every stone in it without favor. The only relief from that came when the little thick-headed Frenchman driving us took it into his head to swing his cattle hard into an overhanging limb or a thorn-bush, which cost my straw bonnet dearly and very nearly cost me my eyes.", "We came through it, though. At the head of the rapids the boat was waiting, loaded and ready for us to get aboard again.", "Our Menomonee guide came up to be presented to me — Wish-tay-yun, a fine, strongly built man with an open, good-humored face that had something not far from mischief in it.", "\"Bon-jour, bon-jour, maman,\" he said, laughing. The French no longer surprised me; I had grown used to that. What surprised me was the respectable title he had decided to give me.", "\"Yes,\" my husband said, \"you must make up your mind to receive a very numerous and well-grown family, consisting of all the Winnebagoes, Pottowattamies, Chippewas, and Ottawas, together with such Sioux, Sacs and Foxes, and Iowas, as have any point to gain in applying to me. By the first-named tribe in virtue of my office, and by the others as a matter of courtesy, I am always addressed as 'father' - you, of course, will be their 'mother.'\"", "Wish-tay-yun and I were friends in no time, my husband turning his Chippewa into English for me as he spoke. The morning was well along by then and we were all impatient to be moving, and everything being in order, the word came:", "\"Pousse au large, mes gens!\" (Push out, my men).", "Just then a boat pulled off from the far bank and came at us across the water. There were white men in it, and they were making signs for us to hold where we were until they could reach us. When they came alongside we found it was Mr. Marsh, a missionary to the Waubanakees, or New York Indians, who had lately been brought into this country, and with him the Rev. Eleazar Williams, who was living at that time among his red brethren on the right bank of the Fox River.", "For men placed as they were — even more than for people in the settlements — the appearance of anyone out of the \"east countrie\" is nothing short of a godsend. We had to hand over every scrap of news we had carried in with us, political, ecclesiastical, and social, and then give a fairly full account of what we meant to do, or rather what we hoped we might be able to do, among our native children at the Portage.", "For my own part I had to admit that I knew next to nothing of the Indian character or the Indian way of living, and that I was going among them with no plan whatever. Good-will in general, and a hope that they would come to be my friends — that was the whole of what I could claim to be carrying. What shape that hope ought to take, and how it might be turned to their greatest good, I would have to let time and better acquaintance teach me.", "Mr. Williams was a dark-complexioned man and a good-looking one. His relations in Connecticut had always spoken of him as \"our Indian cousin,\" so it never entered my head to question that he belonged to that race — though I think now that if I had come across him anywhere else I should have supposed him a Spaniard or a Mexican. There was more of the olive in him than the copper, and his face was grave, very nearly a sorrowful face. He said hardly anything the whole time we talked. He asked few questions and put forward no remark of his own, except when someone had asked him something directly."],
  s15: ["The rapids took everything the men had. Wish-tay-yun's voice was clear and brassy, the bugle the whole crew worked to; he called, and the rest answered him in one throat and threw themselves at the oars again. Then the hull would wedge itself between two great stones and stop dead. \"Whoop la! whoop! whoop!\"", "Another haul. Another. Every nerve in them drawn tight, and nothing moved.", "\"She won't budge!\"", "\"Men, overboard!\" The benches emptied before the words were finished, every rower in the river.", "They shoved and hauled and worried her loose, and then walked alongside her in the shallows, steadying her, steering her, until the water ahead flattened into something like calm. Only then did they climb back aboard and take up the oars.", "There were almost no songs that day. Pipes instead, one after another, the only comfort on offer after work like that.", "I had never watched anything of the kind. Whatever I had seen of the world until then, it had not included men driving their bodies to the far edge of what a body will do, and so the sight worked on me harder than it would have worked on someone used to it. The labor alone seemed punishment enough. But to wade waist-deep in cold water, and then to sit down again in soaked clothes with no fire anywhere near them — poor men, it was more than anyone ought to have to bear. Then I saw my husband. After our noon meal he had startled me by appearing in a pair of duck trowsers and a light jacket, and I had not understood why. At the first shout of \"Fast, again!\" he was over the side with the crew, and he \"bore a hand\" through the whole rest of that long stretch.", "He came back aboard only to take the oar of a boy — one of the company of soldiers, slight and fine-boned, who had bled from the nose since the first day at any effort beyond the ordinary. I asked about him, and was not surprised by the answer: a recruit, only just entered the service. He went by the name of Gridley, though I have no doubt that was a name he had chosen rather than been given. Everything about him said he had been raised gently and had enlisted without the least idea what a soldier's life would ask of him. You could read it in him afterward, too, in the discontent he never once put down, until at last he deserted his post. That was some months after the days I am describing here. They took him once and held him a while in confinement, and the moment they released him he went again. His remains were found the following spring, not many miles from the fort. Cold, or starvation; no one could say which. A grim thing to set down — I will go back to the boats."],
  s16: ["Thirteen miles was all the river had given us since the Kakalin, and we had hauled and strained and paid for every one of them. The light was nearly gone by the time the Grande Chûte came into view, and it was there, close under it, that we were to sleep.", "We had already put the Little Chûte behind us — the place where the town of Appleton stands today — and I noted nothing whatever about it except the sheer quantity of extra labor it cost us to fight that rushing water and come out of the fight the winners.", "The last of the sun lay along the high wooded banks, and down between them came the beautiful foaming rush of the Chûte. The men swung the boat toward a small headland on the left bank, chosen because a long level strip ran back from it, room enough and to spare for a good camp. I was over the side before they had fairly pulled her up, and with the Judge to steady me I went as quickly as I could manage to a point farther down the river, where he promised the Chûte showed itself best. I wanted a sketch of it. I wanted it before the daylight was entirely gone, and there was not much of the daylight left.", "The left bank stood to the west, and where it dipped lower than the rest the parting rays came through and fell on our little scene: the boat, the men in their red caps and belts, the two tents already standing. Smoke was just beginning to lift off the evening fires. Behind all of it rose the high wooded bank with the steep portage path barely traceable up its face, and beyond that, farther off and already darkening under the overhanging forests, the long stretch of the fall. It made a lovely landscape. Only an artist's pencil could have done it any justice.", "It was my first encampment, and the newness of every single thing in it enchanted me.", "The fires were small ones, saplings and underbrush gathered in a hurry; the weather was mild enough that we wanted nothing beyond what would cook a supper and dry the men's clothing. The soldiers had pitched their tent some way off from ours — not so far, though, that we could not hear them perfectly well, laughing, enjoying themselves, after a day that had used them all up.", "Corporal Kilgour kept a careful eye on them, and their high spirits never once crossed out of respectful propriety. Our own supper was cooked in the open air with a tea-kettle and a frying-pan and nothing else at all, and by the time it was eaten there was not one of us who was not ready to lie down.", "What woke us at dawn the next morning was the bourgeois, calling across the camp, a call built to carry:", "\"How! how! how!\" — uttered at the very top of his voice.", "Nobody sleeps through that. The men come out of their tents, or pick themselves up off the ground beside the fire, and begin turning themselves toward the duties of the day.", "The fire is fed. The kettles go on to boil. The mess-baskets are opened and enough is lifted out of them to make a breakfast. One of the Frenchmen spreads our mat inside the tent — the bedding is already up and folded and waiting to be stowed in the boat — and the tin cups and the plates are set out around the new-fashioned table-cloth. The dew is too heavy this morning for us to eat outdoors; on a drier one the whole business would be laid out on the green grass instead. Then, faster than seems possible, the coffee is steaming in front of us and the ham is broiled, and now and again there is toast besides, brought hot and fresh off the glowing coals.", "Say what you please: there is no breakfast on earth like one eaten out in the woods, with a well-trained Frenchman running the ceremony of it.", "What the men turned to that morning was hard labor — getting the heavy boat up the Chûte. The piano was judged safest left where it stood on board, but everything else had to come out and go up the steep bank by hand, then along the top of it for some hundreds of rods, to the smooth water on the far side, where all the difficulties of our navigation came to an end.", "The Judge kindly took me off my husband's hands while \"the bourgeois\" superintended that important business, and between reading and sketching and wandering about, the morning slipped away from me. Twelve o'clock came, and the preparations for starting were still not finished.", "I was out hunting the finest of the wild flowers, meaning to have a bouquet made up before my husband came back, when I walked into the soldiers' camp-fire. A tall young man was minding a kettle over it — red-faced, light-haired, in fatigue dress — and whatever was in that kettle smelled very good indeed.", "He noticed me watching him work, and he ladled out a tin-cupful of it and politely held the cup toward me.", "I said no, and thanked him, and told him our own dinner would be ready directly.", "\"They left me here to get their dinner,\" he said — he seemed glad enough of somebody to talk to — \"and I thought I might as well make some soup. Down on the German Flats, where I come from, they always like soup.\"", "\"Ah! you are from the German Flats - then your name must be Bellinger or Weber.\"", "\"No, it isn't - it's Krissman.\"", "\"Well, Krissman, how do you like the service?\"", "\"Very well. I was only recruited last summer. I used to ride horse on the Canawl, and, as I can blow a horn first-rate, I expect I will soon be able to play on a bugle, and then, when I get to be musician, you know, I shall have extra pay.\"", "I had not known it, but I made all the proper pleased noises anyway, and left him to his kettle with every good wish for his dreams of ambition — though ambition is not quite the word for them. Avarice is nearer the mark. It was plainly the extra pay he had his eye on, and not the fame. Then I went back to my own quarters.", "My husband has French tastes, and he was a little cast down when I told him what had happened and what I had turned away. We were both repaid soon enough. The tall awkward figure came over to us carrying his kettle, set the composition down in front of the two gentlemen, and had the infinite satisfaction of hearing it pronounced excellent.", "When everything was at last in readiness, the tents came down and went around the Portage, and the three of us — my husband, the Judge, and myself — came along behind at our own pace.", "The woods were brilliant with flowers. It was late enough in the season that the glory of the summer was nearly behind us, and still the lupin and the moss-pink and the yellow wallflower were out, and with them every variety of the helianthus, the aster, and the solidago. The gentlemen pulled down clusters of the bittersweet (celastrus scandens) from the boughs overhead and worked them into a wreath for my hat as we went, and the tangled path we went by was Christabel's own, and like hers it was \"Now in glimmer and now in gloom,\"", "through one open glade and then a shady thicket and then another glade. And soon, like that same lovely heroine, \"We reached the place - right glad we were,\"", "and nothing kept us then. We were aboard the little boat again and skimming out over water that had gone perfectly placid."],
  s17: ["The camp we made that night was the finest of the whole journey. The men had been worked hard before noon, so we took only a short stage after it — seven or eight miles of pulling, no more — and put in a little below the \"little Butte,\" where the timber opened out into a beautiful clearing.", "The soldiers went at encamping now like men with a trade. They felled a big tree for the fire and set about cooking food enough for the next day's run, which was to be a long one: twenty-one miles, straight across Winnebago Lake. Our Frenchmen did the same. Then the fire found the light dry grass we were sitting in the middle of, and in a moment the ground all around us was lit and crackling.", "The wind was steady enough to carry the flames one way only, and there was too little to burn for anybody to be frightened by it. We climbed onto the fallen logs and gave that small blaze the grand title of \"a prairie on fire.\" The real loss was that it took every blade of dry grass with it. The night before I had pushed armfuls of that grass under the bear-skin in my tent, and it had made all the difference to my rest.", "Supper was a happy business that evening, eaten at the sunset hour under the high trees, with the water going by and murmuring to itself. Later the moon came up broad and white and poured its light over the whole of it, a country wild and empty and beautiful in its emptiness, and it seemed to me that the friends I had left behind might well envy me.", "But nothing keeps, not even that, and at last my raptures over the scenery ran out with everything else. I shut my tent, unwillingly, and lay down close against the fall of the canvas, where I could lift it a little and look out at the night whenever I chose.", "After a time the camp went quiet. The men had stopped talking and seemed to have gone to their blankets, and we were on the very edge of sleep when a heavy step came up outside, and then a blunt voice with it.", "\"Mr. Kinzie! Mr. Kinzie!\"", "\"Who is there? What is it?\"", "\"I'm Krissman, didn't you mean, sir, that the men should have any liquor tonight?\"", "\"Of course I did. Hasn't Kilgour given out your rations?\"", "\"No: he says you didn't say anything particular about it, and he wasn't coming to ask you if you forgot it, but I thought I wouldn't be bashful - I'd just come and ask.\"", "\"That is right. Tell Kilgour I should like to have him serve out a ration apiece.\"", "\"Thank you, sir,\" in the cheerfullest voice imaginable, \"I'll tell him.\"", "Krissman was becoming a character among us.", "A few miles of rowing the next morning brought us to Four-Legs' village, at the mouth of Winnebago Lake — a handsome scatter of Indian lodges standing about on a green glade under tall, fine trees.", "We were properly in the Winnebago country now. The odd name of the place, I soon learned, came from the head chief of the nation, whose home it was. Nobody was there; they had gone off, in all likelihood, to their wintering grounds. Here we parted with our friend Wish-tay-yun, whose own country ended where this one began.", "\"Bon-jour, Chon!\" (John:) \"bon-jour, maman.\" A hard shake of the hand and that was his goodbye. We pushed off into the lake and left him smoking his kin-nee-kin-nick, waiting for the spirit to move him before he set out at his long Indian trot for home in the Menomonee country.", "Our good weather seemed to leave with him. The sky, clear and mild for days, closed over, and instead of the pleasant crossing we had promised ourselves on that silver water we stayed shut up under canvas, putting our heads out now and then to catch what we could of the country through the pouring rain.", "It was no more than an October day owed us, but we were unreasonable enough to find it tedious. To pass the hours, and to make my disappointment a little smaller, my husband told me something of his early life — a story that belonged, as it happened, to the name Four-Legs.", "He was living then at Prairie du Chien, in the service of the American Fur Company. The chiefs and their people came down from the Upper Mississippi to sell their furs and peltries and to buy what they wanted — merchandise, ammunition, trinkets, and the rest.", "Like everyone not yet seasoned to that country, he took the chills and fever. One day, in a fit of the fever worse than any before it, a chief of the Four-Legs family — brother to the one whose village we had just passed — came into the Company's warehouse to trade. There is no ceremony among the Indians, and no restraint either. Hearing that Shaw-nee-aw-kee was sick, Four-Legs went straight to him to say how sorry he was and to tell him what he ought to be taking for it.", "Anyone who has had the ague, and the fever that comes up behind it, knows what it is to be talked at by a man who cannot see that he should stop. The longer the old chief went on, the higher the fever climbed, and the worse the ache grew in his head and his back and his limbs.", "At last the old man arrived at his point. It was not good, he said, for a young man to be sick and far from his family with no home and no wife. He had a daughter at home, a fine one — handsome, healthy, a capital nurse, and the best hand in the whole tribe at trapping beaver and musk-rats. He would be coming down again in the spring, and he would bring her with him, and then Shaw-nee-aw-kee could see for himself that nothing had been overstated. Should he go now, and bring his daughter next time?", "Beaten flat by that babble, wanting nothing on earth but quiet, poor Shaw-nee-aw-kee agreed to all of it eagerly, and the chief went away.", "The fever ran so close to delirium that the whole conversation went out of his head and stayed out for months. It came back to him on the day Four-Legs walked in with a woman well past her youth and, for ugliness, a perfect Hecate. She had an enormous pack of furs across her shoulders, and she came at him with a lurching, criss-cross walk and dropped the pack at his feet — which, by Indian custom, announced exactly what she understood herself to be to him.", "The talk with her father came back to him whole, and with it the understanding that he was going to need some skill to get himself out of where he now stood.", "He told one of the young clerks to take up the pack and carry it into the magazine where the furs were stored. Then he went on talking with the chief about matters of no consequence, quite coolly.", "Miss Four-Legs sat a while with a sulky, ill-used look on her, and at last it came out of her. \"Humph! he seems to take no more notice of me than if I was nobody!\"", "He turned to the clerk again. \"Give her a calico shirt and half a dozen bread-tickets.\"", "Her face did not clear. Seeing that he would have to open the subject himself, the father said, \"Well, I have brought you my daughter, according to our agreement. How do you like her?\"", "\"Ah, yes - she is a very nice young woman, and would make a first-rate wife, I am sure. But do you know a very strange thing has happened since you were here? Our father, Governor Cass, has sent for me to come to Detroit, that he may send me among the Wyandottes and other nations to learn their customs and manners. Now, if I go, as I shall be obliged to do, I shall be absent two or three years, - maybe four. What then? Why, the people will say, Shaw-nee-aw-kee has married Four-Legs' daughter, and then has hated her and run away from her, and so everybody will laugh at her, and she will be ashamed. It will be better to take some good, valuable presents, blankets, guns, etc., and to marry her to one of her own people, who will always stay by her and take care of her.\"", "The old man was shrewd, and shrewd enough to see that what remained to him was the best bargain he could strike. It cost a round sum, I have no doubt, to settle the thing to the satisfaction of the injured damsel, though I have never been able to learn how much. What I do know is that the young gentleman took care never again to close a bargain in a fit of the ague. And the lady up on the Mississippi is called by his name to this day, in derision.", "Halfway down the lake we passed Garlic Island, a lovely place saddled with an unlovely name. It belonged, with the village on the shore opposite, to \"Wild Cat\" — a fat, jolly, good-natured man, nothing whatever like the formidable animal he was named for.", "He and his band were away, like their neighbors at Four-Legs' village, so there was nothing to break the sameness of the sail. It was too wet for singing. The men sat in their overcoats looking like drowned chickens, and had to work their oars unusually hard simply to keep warm, which likely spared them some of the dead weight of that cold, raining October day. We, who had nothing to do, felt it more.", "Toward evening the sun came out. We had passed into the Fox River and were coming into that pretty little widening called Butte des Morts Lake, at the far end of which we were to camp for the night.", "The shallows along the shore were green with wild rice, and the gathering of it, just at that season, is one of the great labors of the Indian women. They push their canoes into the thick of the stand, bend the heads down over the side with their paddles, and beat the ripe husks off the stalks into a cloth laid ready in the bottom. Afterward the grain is rubbed to free it from the husk, fanned in the open air, put into cordage bags and packed away for the winter. It is longer and more slender than Carolina rice, greenish-olive in color, and though it makes a pleasant enough dish it is far from nutritive. They are fond of it as a soup, with birds or venison added."],
  s18: ["The rain had gone down into the ground and the trees and the brush and stayed there, and there was no camping on any of it, so we made our arrangements to pass the night in our little saloon instead. Getting a fire to take was another matter. But in a wild place that no one has picked over there is always something dry — a broken branch back under the thicket, a tuft of grass the rain never reached — and with the spunk, or light-wood, that no traveler in that country goes without, we had a good blaze going at last, and it was a comfort to us.", "After a day of cold and tedium, it was a fine thing to close in around that fire, to sit on the ends of the burning logs, and watch the Frenchmen build a supper out of it. The kettle went down into a hollow of red coals. Slices of ham went brown and crisping on the forked sticks — broches — that the voyageurs cut and planted around the brands with a few quick strokes of the knife. The pan of pork and onions hissed, and there is no dish a hungry Frenchman would rather be promised. You want a wet, cold day behind you, taken nearly without food, as ours had been, before you know what that one sociable meal, a supper, is really worth.", "The sun went down into great banked masses of cloud, of the kind you see only in our Western sky, and it promised us fair weather for the morning. On that assurance we were glad enough to say goodnight to it, and then, very soon after, to one another.", "We had barely got up next morning at the bourgeois's call when we found that the company had grown in the night. A tall young mitiff — a half-breed — stood there, well made and good to look at, with two or three Indians behind him. The Indians shouted their greetings to their \"father\" and to their new \"mother,\" loud and delighted. They were the first Winnebagoes I had ever seen, and they were, I have to say, by no means the finest specimens of their tribe. The mitiff was one branch of the wide-spreading Grignons, and he had come with an invitation. Judge Law was camped a few miles above us with a friend or two from Green Bay, and would we come and breakfast in his tent? We had never imagined white neighbors so far in. But there is no keeping your whereabouts to yourself while there is one Indian in the country. We waited only for the soldiers to finish eating, and then pushed on for the Butte des Morts — Betty More's, as Mrs. A insisted on calling it to the last.", "The Judge's tent stood white in the morning sun on the little rise we were coming up to. The river under it was crowded with canoes, and it was the squaws doing most of the paddling. Indians stood along the banks with their guns and all their hunting gear, because the whole air overhead was moving with teal, which at that season are everywhere and are the best eating a man can ask. The enormous beds of wild rice here, and in the little lake below, are what bring them in the autumn. That feed fattens them until the flesh comes out white and tender past believing, and it is the reason a young sportsman is warned to fire only at the birds flying low: one killed high up is bruised to pieces by its own fall and is worth nothing on a plate.", "The canoes closed in until we couldn't move, the women all chattering and laughing at once and holding out their little wooden bowls of fresh cranberries to me, an offering to welcome me in.", "I passed the time throwing crackers across to them. Some reached the canoes; some went into the river, and those set off a riot — hands and paddles in the water everywhere at once, and a shout of triumph from whichever of them got to a floating one first.", "Shaw-nee-aw-kee was shaking hands all round with men who plainly counted him an old friend. A good many of them were fine, straight, well-built figures, and you could see in every one a frame made to carry fatigue and the hard usage their manner of life demands. One of them was describing, with a great deal of arm and hand, how thick the game lay just then, and when he was given a quantity of ammunition he was hugely pleased and went off that instant to make his boast good.", "A short walk brought us to the tent, and there I was presented to Judge Law and to a pleasant little gray-haired French gentleman by the name of Porlier. Voyageurs and half-breeds stood about nearby, the voyageurs hard at work, the half-breeds lounging for the most part and doing whatever they did with a sort of listless indifference.", "One class all alive, the other apathetic. Set down side by side that way, the difference between them was striking.", "The party talked a while, breakfast was announced, and we went in and took our seats on the ground around the Indian mat that served for a table.", "The head of it, the post of honor, was given to me, as of course it would be, which let me look round the whole circle of the company and, past them, straight out through the open door of the tent, so that nothing at the side-table outside got by me. Judge Doty sat facing me with his back to the opening, the other gentlemen to the right and left of him. Our waiter was the tall mitiff who had carried the morning's message. He was dressed as he had been then: calico shirt, a bright-colored scarf knotted at the waist, and a straw hat with a band of black ostrich feathers around the crown, which is the usual dress of his class.", "The tin cups came round for our coffee, then breakfast-plates of the same metal, knives and forks after them, and then the food, and the most conspicuous item of it was a large tin pan of boiled ducks.", "The Judge meant to show, I suppose, that a man may sit in the middle of a vast wilderness and keep his standards about him. He picked up the plate in front of him, saw something adhering to it that did not please him, and handed it back over his shoulder to Grignon, asking him to wipe it carefully. Grignon drew out of his bosom a black silk barcelona handkerchief, snugly tucked there against whatever occasion the day might bring, gave the tin a furious polishing with it, and handed it back. The Judge looked it over, smiled his approbation, and glanced round the table as much as to say, \"You see how I choose to have things done,\" and applied himself to his breakfast.", "In those days the trail for Fort Winnebago began on the shore opposite Butte des Morts and ran through the Ma-zhee-gaw-gaw swamp and on past Green Lake. It was fortunate for the Judge that his horses stood waiting for him to mount and away as early as could be managed after breakfast, or I am afraid the story I was tempted to tell would have gone with him and made the whole of that day's ride an uncomfortable one.", "We had hardly finished eating when our hunter came back with the ammunition well accounted for: about fifty fine ducks, shot in little more than an hour. From that morning to the end of our trip we never once wanted for those delicate birds."],
  s19: ["There is a mound at Butte des Morts, and the English of it is the Hillock of the Dead. A battle was fought on that ground long ago between the French and the Mis-qua-kees, whom everyone here calls the Foxes, and the killing ran so far past the ordinary that the rise of earth where the bodies were put down took its name from them and has carried it ever since. Until that day the Foxes had lived along the banks of the river that still goes by their name. They came out of that fight overwhelmed and beaten, and they left the river behind. They went down toward the Mississippi and asked shelter of their allies the Saukies — the Sauks, as they are called now — and year by year the two peoples grew into one another, until the joined tribes came to be known, as they are today, by the single name \"Sauks and Foxes.\"", "The French of the upper country keep a private name for every tribe, each one fastened to some quirk of habit or of body. The Chippewas are light on their feet, so they are \"Sauteurs,\" which is to say Jumpers. The Ottawas are the \"Courtes-oreilles,\" Short-ears. The Menomonees live where the wild rice stands thick, and so they are \"Folles Avoines.\" The Winnebagoes bind the fur of a polecat about their legs when they go out equipped for war, and for that they are \"les Puans.\" The Pottowattamies keep themselves poorly, and are therefore \"les Poux.\" The Foxes are \"les Renards.\" The list runs on in that fashion.", "The consequence is that no French or half-breed resident of the country will name an Indian to you in any other way. \"Such a person is a 'Court-oreille.'\" \"Is that woman a 'Winnebago'?\" \"No, she is a 'Folle Avoine.'\" A newcomer spends his first weeks a good deal puzzled about how to sort the acquaintances he is making.", "Every one of the native friends gathered around us in this place belonged to \"les Puans\" — though the word they use for themselves is far better sounding: the \"Ho-tshung-rahs.\"", "Saying adieu to Judge Doty went hard with me. His company had been the best part of the journey, and his example had taught us a thing worth carrying away, which is to take matters as you find them. We got our leave-taking over not long after breakfast, thanked our kind hosts, and set forward.", "Butte des Morts to the Portage is about seventy miles if you go by land. By water it is not less than a hundred and thirty. The river refuses to run straight — it doubles and coils through the low swampy prairies that lie over so great a part of that country."],
  s20: ["Six miles above the Butte another water comes down to meet the Fox. It is called Wolf River, and it is wide, and it runs straighter and looks more promising than the crooked thing it empties into, which is exactly the trouble with it. Strangers have taken it for the main stream. They have gone up it a long way before learning, much to their chagrin, that every one of those miles would have to be undone.", "Past that junction the river begins to play games with the compass. I was always watching the banks for something worth a sketch, and at one bend I found it: a strip of ground close on the water, broad trees leaning out over it, two or three wigwams tucked in under the shade of them. Bright-looking small children were running about with nothing on at all. Two women sat on the ground, at work on a mat for their lodge. It was a scene worth having, and I began. The bank saw us then and knew Shaw-nee-aw-kee, and the shout went up as it always did — \"Ee-awn-chee-wee-rah, Hee-nee-kar-ray-kay-noo.\"[12] It was one of the occasions on which they let their feeling show. Then we moved on, and I kept working at the drawing.", "The trouble was the sun. It had been squarely behind me when I started. After a while it was off my left shoulder. Then it was straight in front of me. I shifted where I sat, and shifted again, and by and by it had settled on my right hand and stayed there. I could not account for it, and I looked up from the paper to see why. \"Oh, here is a pretty scene. I must have this too! But how surprisingly like the one I have just finished, only in a different direction.\" The laughing came off the bank a second time. It was the same bank. We had passed it not an hour earlier, and we had rowed the better part of four miles since, and all we had to show for that labor was the width of the little shelf of ground the trees and the lodges stood on. I was a traveler in a hurry. The river was not.", "We came out onto Lake Puckaway late in the evening of our second day from Butte des Morts. A white man named Gleason lived there — the one poor Hooe used to aim his little pun at, since nothing Gleason ever told you stayed the size he had told it: \"All isn't gold that Gleasons.\" We did not go to his house. Late in the season as it was, the mosquitoes along that shore were beyond bearing, and we were glad to put ourselves as far off the bank as the water allowed and make our beds in the boat.", "The lake is named for the flags that grow in it, long rushes that grow in its water in great abundance, and out of them the women make the coarse matting that covers a lodge. The way of it is old and wants almost nothing. A woman sits on the ground with the rushes laid side by side and made fast at either end. Her shuttle is a long flat needle cut from bone, with a cord of tree bark run through it, and she drives that needle through one rush and then the next until every stalk is bound hard against its neighbor and the whole is one fine solid mat. They are seldom made longer than five or six feet. Anything bigger is unhandy when a lodge is being set up or taken down.", "The work falls to the older women of a family. When a woman has been worn through — when the weather and the hardship have gone on long enough that she can no longer bring down a tree, or hoe the corn, or carry heavy loads — she is set to weaving mats, and to minding the children, and to keeping order among the dogs, of which there is never any shortage in an Indian lodge.", "Our course took us next into Lac de Boeuf, Buffalo Lake, and it is a lovely sheet of water. In places the shore is very fine, with headlands running out into the clear depths and the great stands of timber crowning them lying upside down in the water as if in a mirror. Now and then a deer would cross one of the glades that opened at intervals in the woodland and be gone. A pair of sand-hill cranes got up ahead of us, beating their wings slowly, looking for somewhere quieter to be. Teal came in flocks, skimming low over the surface and then lifting toward the shelter of the forest, and our sportsman felt every one of them; but a bird brought down in that country would most likely never be found again, and he would not give way to the wanton pleasure of killing for its own sake.", "Then the lake let us out and the river had us back, narrow again, doubling on itself again, and with it went the last of anything worth looking at between there and the end of our trip."],
  s21: ["The country gave us seventy miles and nothing at all to look at. What sharpened the tedium into something worse was knowing the shape of the alternative: with horses, or a carriage of any kind, we could have gone overland from Gleason's to the Portage through pleasant country in a little more than three hours. Even our one dependable comfort was taken from us. The voyageurs' songs need oars, and the river, deep as it is, runs so narrow that in many places there is no room for the regular play of the oars; and a Frenchman can never keep tune unless his oars can keep time. Lapierre tried. He worked away with a paddle — the little row, he called it — and did what he could with it, and it would not go. Then there was the wild rice. In places it grows so thick that it closes the water over entirely, and even a moderate-sized boat must be forced through the tangled masses stroke by stroke, and with difficulty at that. Two days of that, and still the worst of the impatience was ahead of us. It did not arrive until we came in sight of the white walls of Fort Winnebago, looking down from a rising ground over the vast expanse of low land the river winds through.", "The Indians have a tradition about this water. A great serpent once lived in the Mississippi, and took a freak to visit the Great Lakes, and dragged his length across the prairies going; and the trail he left behind collected the water off the meadows and the rain out of heaven as it fell, and in time it became the Fox River.", "The little lakes strung along its course are probably the places where he thrashed about in his uneasy sleep at night. In the neighborhood of the Portage he must have played all the antics of a kitten. When the fort was first pointed out to me I cried out, delighted, \"Oh, we shall be there in half an hour!\"", "\"Not quite so soon,\" my husband said, smiling. \"Wait and see.\" So we sat and watched. We came up on the very bank where we were to disembark — near enough to distinguish the officers standing on it, and a lady with them, waiting to receive us. Then the boat swung, and we had our backs to them, and we were shooting out into the prairie again. After a while another bank came around, with a range of comfortable-looking log houses on it. \"That's the Agency,\" my husband said. \"The largest house belongs to Paquette, the interpreter, and the others are the dwellings of our Frenchmen. The little building, just at the foot of the hill, is the blacksmith's shop, kept there by the Government, that the Indians may have their guns and traps mended free of expense.\"", "\"But are we going to stop there?\"", "\"No — do you not see we are going back to the fort?\"", "And we were. Our course had turned again and we were setting off in our first direction. It went on that way, tacking right and left, putting backward and forward, for the better part of two hours, until at last we came in to the little landing, where the assembled party stood ready to greet us."],
  s22: ["Major and Mrs. Twiggs came out to meet us, and with them a few of the younger officers — nearly all the older ones were away — and our brother Robert, who is known throughout the Indian tribes simply as \"Bob.\" The warmth of that welcome is not a thing I can explain to anyone who has not done what we had just done: travelled a long way to a house set down alone in the wilderness. The Major would not hear of our going to \"the Agency,\" which had been the plan. There were empty quarters inside the fort, and he wanted us in them that hour.", "\"No - we must be under the same roof with them. Mrs. Twiggs had been without a companion of her own sex for more than four months, and would certainly not hear of a separation now. But we must be their guests until the arrival of the boats containing our furniture,\" which was creeping up from Green Bay in the charge of our old acquaintance Hamilton Arndt.", "Dinner was already waiting. That is what the crooked, doubling passage up the Fox River does for you: nobody ever takes their friends by surprise, and dinner can be got ready. The whole circle sat down together, and I do not remember a merrier table.", "Afterward Mrs. Twiggs walked me across the wide hall to see the rooms that were to be ours — two large rooms on each of the building's three floors. The front room on the ground floor stood empty. The one behind it was plainly meant for sleeping, since it held a bedstead of such enormous and ungovernable size that Og, the King of Bashan, might have stretched out on it with Mrs. Og and all the little Ogs beside him. We laughed; we could not help it. And the bedstead was the lesser wonder of that room. Something else stood in the second corner.", "It had been raised under the personal direction of one of our young lieutenants, and he and the soldiers who built it had emptied out every drop of architectural feeling they possessed. The timbers were grooved and carved. The pillars holding up the front bulged and narrowed and bulged again in the most imaginative way. The doors were not merely panelled; they radiated, in a manner calculated to stun any eye that had never seen better. One of these had been put up in every set of quarters, to make good the want of closets — a want that had not once crossed the minds of the bachelors who drew the plans until it was far too late to fix. Each structure had three compartments, intended, one supposes, for clothes-press, store-room, and china-closet; those, at least, were the uses Mrs. Twiggs had found for hers. The only flaw was in the last of the three, where the shelves were set so close together that a gravy-boat could not be got in between them, though what they lacked in space they certainly made up in number. In honor of the man who conceived it we named the whole production a \"Davis,\" and so laid the first laurel on a brow that would later distinguish itself in Cabinet making of an altogether different sort.", "Fort Winnebago stood on a bold shoulder of ground. On one side it looked down over the long reach of prairie and the Fox River; on the other the land ran back into a heavily wooded ridge going off toward Belle Fontaine and Lake Puckaway.", "Out in front was meadow, and cut across the meadow ran the Portage road, some two miles of it, joining the Fox and Wisconsin Rivers. The Government kept teams of oxen at the Agency, and a driver with them, for hauling the Indians' canoes over that ground — which through much of the year was soaked and miry and very nearly impossible to cross at all."],
  s23: ["The woods had turned. Every tint of autumn was in them at once, and the ground under them was full of movement — groups of Indians in every direction, lodges pitched here and there around the Agency buildings. On the low ground below stood the white tents of the traders. They had come early and set up already, stocked to furnish the winter supplies, and waiting on the annuity money that was about to pass into Indian hands and straight back out of them again.", "A great concourse had been gathering for days. They were waiting on the payment, and the payment was waiting on Shaw-nee-aw-kee, who was bringing the silver.", "Before any of it could begin, the great chief of the nation had held a carouse. Four-Legs, whose village we had passed at the mouth of Winnebago Lake. It is too apt to be the custom when the tribes come into the neighborhood of a sutler's establishment, and in this case the means were more than usually ready to hand, because there were traders on the ground who held the very stringent laws against selling liquor to the Indians as no particular obstacle.", "Four-Legs could not stand this full tide of prosperity. His Father the agent was not there to check him, and there was nothing else in the way, and he carried his indulgence so far that within a few days it killed him. They had buried him with unusual pomp the day before we arrived, and my chief feeling, when I learned it, was disappointment at having come too late to witness the ceremonies.", "They had wrapped the body in a blanket, as their custom is, and laid it in a rude coffin along with his guns, his tomahawk, his pipes and a quantity of tobacco. Then they carried it up to the highest point of the hill facing the fort, an immense procession of his people coming behind it, whooping, beating their drums, howling — making altogether what is emphatically called a pow-wow.", "When the body was in the ground they planted a stake at its head, painted over in vermilion with a series of hieroglyphics setting out the great deeds and events of his life. Around the whole of it they drove pickets cut from the trunks of tamarack-trees. To that enclosure his friends would come for many days together, to renew the expression of their grief and to throw tobacco and other offerings over the grave to the Great Spirit.", "There was one consolation in having come late. We were still in time to furnish a quantity of white cotton for a flag to wave over the grave, and to settle a considerable bill at the sutler's for the various articles the funeral parade had been found to require — it being a duty expected of their Father to bury the dead suitably.", "The observances were not finished with him. All that day, and then all that night, the sound of their instruments came across to us mixed with lamentation, and with the discordant whoops and yells of men who were still partly drunk, and there was no sleeping through it. Every so often something else threaded through the noise: the plaintive notes of the Indian flute, which a young man plays when he is in love. Grief and whiskey had made their hearts tender, and the woods gave back their melancholy strains until morning."],
  s24: ["Early the next morning, before I had even left my room, it reached me from the apartment next door — lamentation, woe. I went in. Several squaws were sitting on the floor with their eyes cast down, their whole posture condolence and sympathy, and in the middle of them sat a little ugly woman in tattered clothes, her face blackened, her hair loose and disordered, sobbing and wailing bitterly.", "They had to be the family of the dead chief. It troubled me badly that I had no way of telling them I shared in their sorrow except with my hands.", "I knew nothing whatever of their customs. Looking at how wretched they were, I took it for granted that part of what afflicted them was plain poverty and destitution. One of them at least appeared to be at the very bottom of misery. \"Can it be possible,\" I said to myself, \"that this poor creature has only these scanty rags to cover her?\"", "I stepped back into my room and came out with a pretty calico wrapper, and I put it into the hands of the little dirty blackened object in the middle of the floor. She took it, and began a fresh round of sobbing and sighing. I made signs that she should put it on. I opened it out for her, showed her how the thing was meant to be worn, and urged her with gestures to waste no time at all in getting herself more comfortable.", "At this, the other women burst out laughing.", "\"Very mal-à-propos,\" thought I, \"and somewhat unfeeling.\" Then my husband came in and explained it to me. The widow, Madame Four-Legs, was the chief mourner. She had, beyond any doubt, a perfectly comfortable wardrobe waiting at home; going for a season neglected, with a blackened face, is simply what the etiquette of mourning requires. He told me all of this in the gaps between handshakes, while condolences were offered and returned in a language more uncouth and guttural than any I had heard in my life. Their Father dismissed them at last with a promise of presents to help dry up the tears. Let nobody conclude from any of that the poor little widow's grief was not sincere. She had been greatly attached to her husband, and her influence had been great — over him, and over the whole nation besides. A Fox woman, she spoke the Chippewa, and the Chippewa is the court language of every tribe, so people came to her constantly to interpret; and she had made a practice of travelling with her husband and helping him with her counsels on every occasion that arose. She had great shrewdness and judgment. She also had, as I found out for myself later on, affections that were strong and would not let go."],
  s25: ["When breakfast was over the principal chiefs came to call on me. They had turned out in their best clothing and their best paint, to look at their new mother.", "Naw-kaw was among them, Kar-ray-mau-nee, \"the Walking Turtle,\" the head chief of the nation now — a big-framed man with a broad, agreeable face, and the whole of that face governed by an enormous under lip that hung down almost to his chin. Old Day-kau-ray came too, and there was no one more noble or more venerable in his own tribe, or in any tribe I have known. He had a fine Roman face, and his bald head made it finer, with one single tuft of long silver hair tied neatly and lying back over his shoulders. His dress was exact and proper and almost without ornament of any kind, and his courtesy never went off him, whatever the circumstances. All of it together put him first in the regard of everyone who knew him. What comes later in this book will show that what he was made of stood as high as the way he looked and carried himself.", "Black-Wolf was there, and his name told you his face: heavy, surly, lowering. What sharpened it was the mass of black hair hanging loose all around it, which is not the Winnebago fashion at all. They generally take off a part of the hair and draw the rest back, clubbing it behind and dressing it with beads, ribbons, cock's feathers — or, where the man has earned it, one eagle's feather for every scalp he has taken from an enemy.", "Talk-English was there, a strikingly handsome and powerful young man, and this is how he got the name. He had been one of a party of sixteen Winnebagoes who went the year before, on invitation, with their Agent and Major Forsyth — the Chippewa, they called him — to see the President at Washington.", "All along the road, people who had never met a Western Indian asked them the same thing: \"Do you talk English?\"", "The young man missed nothing, and he took the question to his Father. \"What do they mean by this? Everybody says to me, talk English!\"", "The Agent translated it for him. \"Ah, very well.\"", "The next stop they made was Lockport, in the State of New York. He jumped off the canal-boat onto the lock and went straight at the first man he saw, pushing his face forward. \"Talk Eengeesh?\"", "\"Yes,\" said the man, \"do you talk English?\"", "\"Ya-as.\"", "He carried the name from that day on, and it went onto the pay-rolls as his title, and he was not a little proud of it.", "Hoo-wau-ne-kah, \"the Little Elk,\" was another of the great men of that tribe, and had also made the Washington journey. Henry Clay came to see the deputation, studied every face and every bearing in the room, and then pointed to that one man as the one with the most ability in him. He was much gratified to be told that he had picked out the principal orator of the nation, a man decidedly above every other individual of his tribe in talent.", "Wild-Cat I have spoken of already — our Indian Falstaff, cowardice and falsehood excepted.", "Kau-ray-kaw-saw-kaw was there as well, \"the White Crow,\" a Rock River Indian, who later made a name for himself as a friend to the whites through the Sauk war. The French called him \"le Borgne,\" because he had lost an eye, and he wore a black silk handkerchief drooping over the left side of his face to cover the loss, which, with the rest of his native dress, made him a very singular figure to look at.", "There was a nephew of the dead chief Four-Legs whom whites and Indians alike called \"the Dandy,\" and no one ever disputed the justice of it. When he was out of mourning his dress was as studied and as fanciful as he could contrive it. A shirt, on the days he condescended to wear one, in the brightest colors going, covered with row upon row of silver brooches set close together. Never fewer than two pairs of silver arm-bands. Leggings and moccasins worked in ribbon and porcupine-quill embroidery of the most elaborate kind. Every ornament he could invent hung off the club of hair at the back of his head. A feather fan in one hand, and in the other a mirror, into which he looked at himself about every five minutes. Add the colors on his face, in their variety and brilliancy, the choosing and laying on of which took up no small share of the hours he gave to his toilet, and that was young Four-Legs, complete.", "In a young dandy all that devotion to appearance seems natural enough. But we had an old one of exactly the same stamp. Pawnee Blanc, the White Pawnee, went past his younger rival in the care of his personal attractions, if such a thing were possible.", "He arrived that day in the whole of his finery, made the customary salutations with an air of solemn dignity, and then walked through, as the rest of them did, into the parlor — I had received them in the hall — where they all sat themselves down on the floor. It was lucky for me that the room stood bare of furniture just then. But \"alas!\" thought I, \"for my pretty carpet, if this is to be the way they pay their respects to me!\" I sat and watched the ash dropping from those long pipes, and the other inconveniences that come with tobacco, or kin-nee-kin-nick, in absolute dismay.", "After the chiefs came the interpreter and his wife, and with them all the Canadian and half-breed women whose husbands had work at the Agency or at the American Fur Company's establishment.", "By that time my piano had been got out of its case and set up in our quarters, and to our great joy there was not a mark on it. Thanks to the skill of Nunns and Clark, not one note had gone out of tune.", "The women had never seen such a thing in their lives, and their wonder and delight came out of them at the top of their voices.", "\"Eh-h-h! regardez donc! Quelles inventions! Quelles merveilles!\" One of them caught sight of my fingers moving in the reflection on the nameboard and called out to the others in triumph. She believed she had found the hidden machinery that made the sounds, and she was not a little mortified when she learned better."],
  s26: ["The boats were only a few days off, so it seemed sensible to start on whatever part of the housekeeping I could manage without them. That came down to cutting and stitching my carpets, and Mrs. Twiggs was kind enough to sit and sew with me. The wife of one of our Frenchmen came over from the Agency and put the rooms to rights and made them comfortable, and the carpets went down under tacks not long after. After that the rooms stood empty and waiting for the rest of the furniture.", "Getting a servant-woman to come out with me had defeated me over and over, first in Detroit and then at Green Bay. Now and then one would offer herself. Then, before anything was settled between us, something would work on her — how far it was, or the savages, or what the trip itself would be like, or, as often as not, what her friends had to say about it — and the bargain would come apart in my hands. In the end I had nothing to fall back on but my husband's assurance that one of his French employés and the man's wife might be willing to live under our roof with us.", "That was where the matter stood when Major Twiggs offered a way around all of it. He had a young colored girl in his household, brought up from Buffalo in the spring to wait on Mrs. T. until her own servants could come north from the South, and he proposed that we take the girl into our service.", "So Louisa was sent for. She was an uncommonly handsome young negress with an intelligent face that she kept very demure, and she gave her age as fifteen. She cannot have been fifteen. What I learned later of the distance she had already travelled into vice and iniquity would have taken several more years than that. But none of that was visible to me then. Then she seemed to have nothing wrong with her but carelessness and inexperience, and I had every hope that careful training would cure her of both.", "My first week's visit at Mrs. Twiggs's had just run out when the word went round that the boats were in sight — the boats with our furniture in them. If Louis Philippe had been expected on a visit to Queen Victoria the sensation could hardly have been greater than the one that announcement made in our little community. We knew perfectly well that hours had to pass before they could reach the place where they would unload, and we watched anyway, constantly. In the end every young officer in the garrison, with as many of the soldiers as were off duty behind them, went down the bank to the landing with Mr. Kinzie, to see the thing done and to lend a hand if a hand was wanted getting it all safely ashore.", "What came ashore was in a sad state. Water ran out of the corners of the boxes as they were swung up one after another onto the bank. Nobody had the patience to wait for them to be carried up to the fort. The gentlemen found themselves hammers and hatchets and went at the lids then and there, to learn how bad it was. The mahogany was ruined. There was not one piece of it whose edges and veneering had not begun to lift and curl away. It looked as though it had lain days under the Grande Chûte. Poor Hamilton was loud with his protests and his excuses.", "The men had done it. The weather had done it. The packing had done it. \"Confound it! he had taken the best care of the things he possibly could - better than he had ever taken before - it would get done!\"", "There was nothing for it but patience and making do. And when the pretty sideboard and the work-table had been rubbed and rubbed and set on their feet, and the little knick-knacks stood along the mantel-piece, and the white curtains were up at the windows, and the chairs and the dining-table had each found its place with respect to the piano, our parlor was pronounced \"magnificent.\" Hamilton certainly thought so. He came up to look at it once, admiringly, and to hear the piano played, which was a thing entirely new to him. What he told the young officers about it after he got back to the Bay carried all his wonder in it — \"There it stood on its four legs! Anybody might go up and touch it!\"", "In time everything found a home. The dinner- and tea-sets were bestowed carefully in the \"Davis,\" along with the jars of sweetmeats I had put up in Detroit. The iron and tin things went into a neat kitchen cupboard whose frame was my piano-box. The barrel of eggs and the tubs of butter, carried the whole way from Ohio, were ranged in the store-room. We bought a suitable quantity of salt pork and flour from the commissary, and of game there was never any shortage of any kind, that being the offering of our red children. We were ready to keep house.", "A young housekeeper's first dinner in her own home is an era, and I will never forget mine. I was down in the lower regions standing over my very inexpert little cook when my husband appeared to tell me that the payment — which was the one subject anybody could think about just then — would not begin until the afternoon, and that he had accordingly asked M. Rolette, Mr. Hempstead, and four other gentlemen to dine with us.", "\"So unexpected - so unprepared for?\"", "\"Never mind, give them anything you have. They have been living for some days in tents, and anything will taste well to them.\"", "I had meant the dinner to be mostly a venison pasty, and I was saved by the single dish in my possession large enough to be worth mentioning, because there was already a pie smoking in the oven that came near the size of the famous Norwich pudding. A few small additions to the bill of fare and we came off perfectly well, and the master of the house had the pleasure of hearing his six guests praise that impromptu dinner a great deal."],
  s27: ["The Winnebago were paid in two divisions. One of them came to the Agent at the Portage; the other went to General Street at Prairie du Chien. Ours numbered somewhere between four and five thousand people, and under the treaty stipulations they were owed fifteen thousand dollars a year, along with a considerable quantity of presents, and so many rations of bread and pork besides, to be given out at need across the rest of the year.", "Their principal villages lay scattered wide: at Lake Winnebago; at Green and at Fox Lakes; at the Barribault; at Mud Lake; at the Four Lakes; at Kosh-ko-nong; at Turtle Creek. When the annuity-money was on its way, or had just come in, messengers rode out to all of them with word that the heads of families and lodges were to gather at \"the Portage.\"", "They came in by band, each master of a family under his own chief, and gave his name and the number of people in his lodge to be written down. Payment was reckoned by the head, so a man had a plain interest in making his lodge look as large as the register would allow. Each one brought a small bundle of sticks and handed it over to the Agent to be counted. What followed sometimes went like this:", "\"How many have you in your lodge?\"", "The man counts through his bundle, slowly, with a great deal of ceremony - \"Fifteen\"", "\"How many men?\"", "\"Two.\" Two sticks are laid to one side \"How many women?\"", "\"Three.\" Three more are set apart.", "\"How many children?\"", "\"Eight\" Eight sticks join the heap.", "\"What is the meaning of these two sticks that stay?\"", "And the man whose arithmetic has not been equal to his ambition goes, off through a crowd of his own people hooting and laughing after him — nothing delights them more than a piece of roguery found out that they had no part in.", "The young officers usually helped count the money out and hand it over, and it happened often enough that when the last band came forward the chiefs would take up a handful of the silver from the box and ask their Father to see his friends paid for the trouble they had taken; his refusing it genuinely distressed them. It was of a piece with everything else about them. That courtesy, that politeness, is never once let go of. Let a party come and beg provisions of their Father, and be offered food, and however hungry they may be, every one of them waits while a single man divides the whole into equal shares, and then takes his own portion and eats it quietly and with the greatest moderation. I saw that rule broken exactly once.", "Our friend Pawnee Blanc — the Old Dandy — came one afternoon with a party who asked leave to dance for us on the open ground in front of the door. The day was warm and dusty, the dancing was violent and went on a long while, and when they were thoroughly heated and worn out with it a pitcher of raspberry negus was made up and carried out to them. Pawnee took charge of the pitcher and the tumbler. He filled the tumbler about halfway and handed it to the first man of the circle, filled it the same for the next, and went on down the line — and then he thought to look down into the pitcher. What he found in there settled his course of action. He set the tumbler on the ground, raised the pitcher in both hands to his lips, and took a long and hearty pull at it. Then he resumed, pouring less, and then less again, until somebody called for the pitcher to be replenished. Every person present agreed it was the only time any of them had seen an Indian look afraid of coming away with less than his share.", "Whiskey gets into the lodges during a payment — a good many kegs of it — and neither the officers nor the Agent can watch closely enough to keep it out. Where there is a demand there will be a supply, and the legal prohibitions may say what they like. The last day of a payment is, without fail, given over to carousing of the general kind.", "Once the men are well into their frolic, the women move through and collect every gun, knife, tomahawk and weapon of whatever description, and put them out of sight, so that as little mischief as possible gets done while restraint and reason are absent. I am sorry to have to set it down that our little friend Pawnee Blanc loved the bottle far too well.", "Among the presents Shaw-nee-aw-kee had brought back from the East for the chiefs were a trunk of blue coats in cloth, laced broadly in gold, and a box of round black hats got up in the same manner. Anyone who knows Indians, of whatever tribe, has noticed that the first step toward civilization, in a man or in a woman, is mounting a man's hat and trimming it with tinsel or ribbon or feathers. The distribution remembered Pawnee among its happy number. He put the whole costume on at once, tied a few more bunches of gay-colored ribbon to the long spear he carried always as his baton of ceremony, and came straight over to pay me a visit of state, with an admiring train at his back, most of them women.", "There was a wonderful solemn gravity on his face as he motioned away anyone who came close enough to finger the new finery, and he strutted, and he edged aside from this person and that so that no homely, every-day wardrobe should brush him in passing. All of it argued a long reign of propriety, of self-respect, of a due regard for the good opinion of everybody around him. But alas for Pawnee. Late in the day he came past us on the way to his lodge, held up between two stout young men who had just hauled him up out of a ditch. The fine coat was plastered with mud, the hat was battered and bruised, the spear had been shorn of every gay streamer, and Pawnee himself was weeping and uttering every doleful lamentation known to a tipsy Indian."],
  s28: ["One of the first women I came to know was the wife of Wau-kaun-zee-kah, the Yellow Thunder. She had gone east with her husband when he went as one of the deputation to visit the President, and ever since she had been called \"the Washington woman.\" She greeted me pleasantly, the way one old acquaintance greets another, as much as to say that she and I had both seen something of the world. While her companions went through my things with a childlike, laughing simplicity, crying out and clapping their hands at every wonder I brought forward, she let nothing at all cross her face. Her whole bearing said it for her: yes, yes, my children, I have seen these things already. The apathy held until I took a tropical shell out of the little cabinet I kept them in and held it to her ear. She heard the murmuring inside it. She pushed her finger down into the opening, after the animal inside it. She shook the shell hard. She put it back to her ear and listened again. Then she laughed out loud, and set it down, and her face admitted freely that this one was past her.", "There was one shell in the collection I loved above all the others, a small conch with rich dark veins running through it. Every woman there picked that one up in her turn, and every one of them said and gestured how beautiful she found it; they had an eye for it, plainly. This was on the day my red daughters came to take their leave of me.", "The payment was finished soon after and the Indians went away, and it was then I noticed the conch was gone from the cabinet. Had one of the squaws taken it? It was not impossible. Under a strong enough temptation they would do such a thing now and then, though rarely. I went back over the faces of that afternoon, trying to fix on the likeliest one. Not the Washington woman, certainly. She was partly civilized, and knew better.", "Some weeks later Mrs. Yellow Thunder walked in again with a gay-colored chintz shawl rolled up in her hand. She unfolded it carefully, took out my shell, and set it down on the table in front of me. I could not decide whether my face ought to show displeasure at the trick or gladness at having my treasure back, and settled at last on showing nothing whatever.", "She stayed on and on, until my husband came home, and then he asked her about it.", "\"She had taken the shell to her village, to show to some of her people, who didn't come to the payment.\"", "\"Why had she not asked her mother's leave before carrying it away?\"", "\"Because she saw that her mother liked the shell, and she was afraid she would say, No.\"", "That was not the first proof I had of Madame Washington's shrewdness, which was the leading thing in her character. On the trip to the Eastern cities the party had been taken about to exhibitions, museums, menageries, theatres, and it did not get past them that a piece of silver changed hands at every door before anyone was let in. They asked why. It was explained. At that the woman brightened, like a person who has just had an idea.", "\"How much do you pay for each one?\"", "Her Father told her.", "\"How do you say that in English?\"", "\"Two shillings.\"", "\"Two shinnin - humph\" (good).", "The next day the visitors came flocking as usual to the rooms where the Indians were lodged, and found the door shut. The woman had posted herself beside it with a young Indian for a confederate. At each knock the door was opened cautiously, a hand came out, and a voice said, \"Two shinnin.\"", "Every caller paid without argument, and it went on that way until she had gathered up a considerable sum. Even that was not enough for her. When the first visitor made a move toward the door to go, the door was held fast, the hand came out again, and again he heard \"Two shinnin.\" He tried to explain that a man who has paid to come in goes out for nothing. She shook her head, all innocence. \"Two shinnin\" was the whole of the English she could understand.", "The Agent had come in a little while before and heard the entire performance from behind his newspaper, where he sat laughing and waiting to see how far it would run. Now he stepped in and put an end to it, and the guests were let out without any further contribution.", "The good woman was told, besides, that white people do not tax their friends and visitors in this fashion, and that she would have to give the practice up.", "Here is another instance of how readily the Indians helped themselves to whatever good things fortune laid in their path.", "On that same journey, somewhere in Ohio, one of the party put a question to the Agent. \"Do you pay for all those provisions that are set before us at the hotels?\"", "\"Yes. Why do you ask?\"", "\"Nothing: I thought you maybe paid for just what we ate of them.\"", "At the next stopping-place a handsome breakfast was laid out, and they ate of it plentifully, as they always did. They had just finished when the horn blew for everyone to take their seats in the stage-coaches. Every man was on his feet at once. One took up the plates of biscuits and poured them into a corner of his blanket. Another took what was left of a pair of chickens. A third turned out the sugar-bowls. Each of them laid hands on whatever stood nearest, and in a trice the table held nothing but empty plates and dishes. The landlord and the waiters watched the whole thing and laughed as hard as anybody else in the room.", "Another time, their Father had done his best to impress on them how unseemly it was to drop their bones and scraps and leavings about on the table-cloth. He pointed out how orderly white people were at table, and how proper it was to keep everything neat and clean about one.", "At the very next meal they were served a chicken-pie first, and ate it with a will, and the bones came up in great numbers on their plates. Then a second dish arrived, one they liked better still — a fine large roasted turkey. A gentleman sitting near it had taken up the carving knife. There was no time to lose, and there was still the question of the bones. They looked about them, at something of a loss. A large apple-pie stood within reach. The quickest of them pulled it over, and in the space of a thought every bone was piled onto it, and, laughing in triumph at his own happy idea, he lifted the bird calmly onto his own dish and carried it off to share out among his companions. The astonished stranger was soon laughing too, at the very unceremonious way his share of the dinner had disappeared."],
  s29: ["The payment was over and the Indians had scattered to their wintering grounds. The traders went too, carrying off a good share of the silver; against it every family had laid in what it could — clothing, guns, traps, ammunition, whatever else the winter would call for. The Indians are hard to get the better of in a bargain. They know an exorbitant price the moment they hear one, and a trader who tries his shrewdness on them earns a name for it, an apt one, which stays on him ever after.", "M. Rolette, for one, they called \"Ah-kay-zaup-ee-tah,\" which is to say five more. The reason they gave was that a man might offer him any number of skins he pleased for an article and the terms would come back the same every time: \"five more\"", "A lady said to him once, \"Oh, M. Rolette, I wouldn't be engaged in the Indian trade, it seems to me a system of cheating the poor Indians.\"", "He answered her with great naïveté. \"Let me tell you, madame, it isn't so easy a thing to cheat the Indians as you imagine. I have tried it these twenty years, and have never succeeded!\"", "We settled into a quiet domestic life. The military system divided the day up for us: the bugle-call at reveille, with a very good band after it; the light, quick strains for sick-call, and a little later for breakfast; the longer business of guard-mounting; \"Old English Roast-Beef\" to say that dinner was ready; at retreat the sweet, plaintive \"Lochaber no more,\" followed as incongruously as could well be managed by \"The Little Cock-Sparrow\"; and late in the evening the long roll of tattoo. Between those markers, with books and music and drawing on top of the household work, the hours went faster than they ever had at home. Sunday was the day I missed my Eastern home most. I had settled well in advance what we should do when the first of them came round in a house of our own. \"We shall have at least,\" I said to myself, \"the Sabbath's quiet and repose, and I can, among other things, benefit poor Louisa by giving her some additional lessons of a serious character.\"", "So, as she was clearing away the breakfast-things, I told her, \"Now, Louisa, get your work all finished, and everything put neatly aside, and then come here to me again.\"", "\"Yes, ma'am.\"", "We opened our books. We read, and waited. We read another hour and waited. No Louisa.", "There was music out on the parade below our windows, and voices with it, but we heard as much every day of our lives and thought nothing of it.", "In the end I had to go down myself and find out what was keeping my damsel. The kitchen was empty. The breakfast-things stood on the table still, the kettles and the spider still on the hearth, and the fire had gone out.", "I went back into the hall, which runs the whole length of the house and opens at the front on the parade, and there was a crowd gathered in the area, of every shade and color. In the middle of it was one round woolly head I could not possibly mistake, bobbing to this side and then to that, and peals of laughter were coming out of the whole group.", "\"Louisa,\" I called, \"come here. What are you doing there?\"", "\"Looking at inspection.\"", "\"But why aren't your breakfast-things washed, and your kitchen swept?", "Did I not tell you I wished you to come up and learn your lessons?\"", "\"Yes, ma'am, but I had to see inspection first. Everybody looks at inspection on Sunday.\"", "An afternoon lesson was plainly all I was ever going to get out of Louisa, and I made up my mind to be content with that.", "It struck me that it would be a pleasant thing, and perhaps a profitable one, if everybody in the garrison gathered together on that day. Somebody among us, surely, would be willing to read out a portion of the church-service, and a sermon chosen from one of our different selections.", "I opened the subject carefully, by way of a question:", "\"Are there none among the officers who are religiously disposed?\"", "\"Oh, yes,\" came the answer from the one I had asked, \"there is S - -, when he is half tipsy, he takes his Bible and 'Newton's Works,' and goes to bed and cries over them, he thinks in this way he is excessively pious.\"", "S - - was one of the officers who had never called on us. If his religious principles had done nothing to correct his own evil habits, it was fair to suppose they would not do much toward improving anybody else's, and there seemed no use in asking his help with any scheme for keeping the Lord's day better.", "So we wrote to our friends at the East instead, and asked them to take up the business of getting a missionary sent out who could serve as chaplain to the garrison. The officers appeared to like the plan. For the present we set aside any hope of united religious services.", "The post-surgeon took a furlough, and Dr. Newhall, of Galena, came out in his place; with his gentle, quiet wife added to us, our circle of ladies came to three. A wilderness — and yet how contented and happy we were.", "The gloom that ended that enviable quiet arrived in the person of a Frenchman named Letendre, who presented himself one day without warning. He had come from Chicago to tell us that our dear relative Dr. Wolcott was gravely ill; hopelessly ill, in fact. My husband began at once to get ready to leave. I begged him to take me, but he meant to travel too fast for that and had to refuse me. Within a few hours the provisions and the horses and everything else needed for the trip were assembled, and he rode off with Petaille Grignon, who always attended him on such expeditions, leaving Letendre to follow when he had recovered from his fatigue.", "The hours of his absence were sad and dreary ones, for all that our friends did to cheer me. Within a few days another messenger came with word that Dr. W.'s illness had ended fatally. That noble heart, full of warm and kindly affection as it was, had stopped, and those who had loved and honored him were left sad and desolate indeed.", "My husband came back as soon as he could leave the family, and it was well he did not put it off any longer, because the winter set in then, and it set in hard.", "Our quarters were spacious, but the soldiers had built them out of green trees felled in the forest and sawed into boards by hand, and the boards shrank and warped and left gaping cracks everywhere. Many a leisure hour went into stuffing them with cotton batting and pasting paper strips over the top.", "The chimneys smoked, too, and steadily, for all the currents of air that might have been expected to give them a draught. The commanding officer's remedy was to have a pair of gun-barrels sawed off and set into the hearth, one at each side of the fire-place, on the theory that air coming up out of the room below would help push the smoke where it belonged.", "Louisa was washing the hearth the next morning, after they had been put in.", "\"Pray, ma'am,\" she said, \"what are these things put in here for?\"", "I explained their use.", "\"Oh, I am so glad it is only that! Uncle Ephraim (Major Twiggs's servant) said they were to be filled with powder and fired off Christmas Day, and he was terribly afraid they would blow the house up, and we in it.\"", "Ephraim was a most faithful and valuable servant, and he entertained himself by playing on the credulity of the younger sort among the colored fraternity.", "\"Is it true,\" asked Louisa, one day, \"that Pillon and Plante were once prairie-wolves?\"", "\"Prairie-wolves! what an idea! Why do you ask such a foolish question?\"", "\"Because Uncle Ephraim says they, and all the Frenchmen about here, were once prairie-wolves, and that, living so near the white people, they grow, after a time, to be like them, and learn to talk and dress like them. And then, when they get to be old, they turn back into prairie-wolves again, and that all the wolves that the officers bait with their dogs used to be Frenchmen, once.\"", "In time I gave up setting Uncle Ephraim's stories straight. I was coming round by degrees to the view that my little colored damsel was nothing like as simple and unsophisticated as she wished me to believe her, and that the one being imposed upon was me.", "The snow that winter was prodigious and the cold was intense. Water froze in our parlors a very short way from the fire. The fatigue-parties kept the halls stacked with wood nearly to the ceiling, but that was no answer to having the wide doors standing open on the outer air for a great part of the day while they carried it in. Northerners as we were, we ought to have had wood-houses built for the purpose, and kept the great hall-doors shut, and put a \"hurricane-house\" over them besides. But the Florida frontier is no place for a Southern bachelor to pick up the sort of knowledge that comes in useful when the thermometer stands at twenty-five degrees below zero — the point at which the brandy in the sideboard congealed."],
  s30: ["Christmas and New Year brought our Ho-chunk friends back to the Portage. They had learned something of the holidays from their French neighbors, and I had been warned in advance that I would see the women kissing every white man they met. I did not entirely believe that. I did believe the rest of it — that each of them would expect a present, a compliment of the season — so I laid in a supply of beads, ribbons, combs, and whatever other small bright things I had. They were fond of dainties, all of them. The day before, I had a great quantity of crullers and doughnuts fried, so there would be something ready in the house that they would actually want.", "The frying had been left to Louisa. When the cakes came up to be put away in the \"Davis,\" only a moderate share of them were there, which surprised and annoyed me.", "\"Where are the rest of the cakes, Louisa?\"", "\"That great fellow, Hancock, came in with the fatigue-party to fill the water-barrels, and while I had just stepped into the store-room to get some more flour, he carried off all I had got cooked.\"", "She made a face and whined it out, as if she had not herself treated every soldier who ever set foot on the premises.", "Early the next morning I had quite a levee of Ho-tshung-rah matrons. They came in one after another and seated themselves in a circle on the floor, and I was sorry to see that soap and water had formed no part of their holiday preparation where their blankets were concerned. There was no one to interpret. I thought I would open the talk in a language they would understand, so I took a china dish from the sideboard, filled it with the brown crullers, grated a good quantity of white sugar over them as one does, and handed it to the first woman in the circle. She took the dish out of my hand, tipped every cake into the corner of her blanket, and gave the dish back to me empty. She must be a very hungry woman, I thought. I will manage better next time. I filled the dish again and went to the second, keeping fast hold of the rim so that she could take one modestly. She scooped the whole of it out with both hands and put the cakes away in her blanket like the first. My sense of politeness rebelled at doling them out one at a time the way you do with children, so I sat down to think, because the supply would clearly not stretch to that kind of demand, and there would be more visitors before long.", "While I sat there at a loss, the ones who had the cakes began sharing them out, and the whole number was divided fairly around the circle. But they did not eat them. They ran their fingers over the grated sugar, looked into one another's faces, and muttered low. There was something here they did not understand. At last one, bolder than the rest, wet her finger, picked up a few grains, and put it carefully to her mouth.", "\"Tah-nee-zhoo-rah!\" — Sugar! — she said, delighted, and the whole room broke into a hearty laugh. The cakes went after that as fast as good breeding allowed. They had never seen any sugar but the brown or yellow maple. They had taken the white for salt, and that was why not one of them would taste it.", "They stayed on and on, until Shaw-nee-aw-kee at last made his appearance, and then, having been made happy by their various gifts, they all took their leave together."],
  s31: ["A letter reached Mr. Kinzie around this time from Colonel Richard M. Johnson, of Kentucky. The Colonel had thrown himself with great warmth behind a school got up in that State for the schooling of Indian youths and children, and he wanted something from the Agent. He wanted him to work on the Winnebagoes — to press them to send their children there, and, what mattered a good deal more to the Colonel, to press them to lay aside some share of their annuity-money each year to keep the place running.", "It was the holiday season, and a good many of the chiefs happened to be camped near the Portage. A messenger went out to gather them in at Paquette's house — Paquette was the interpreter — so that their Father could talk with them there.", "They came, all of them, on the day named. The letter was opened out before them, and everything that could be said for civilization and education was said: what it would mean for the whole nation if even a handful of the younger ones were properly taught by the whites and then sent home again to pass on to their people the learning, the arts, the manufactures, the habits of a civilized life. At the end of each point, as it was rendered to them, the answer came back from all of them together — \"Humph!\" (Good!)", "Their Father finished. Then Day-kau-ray, the oldest of the chiefs there and the one they held in most reverence, got to his feet and said this:", "\"Father, - The Great Spirit made the white man and the Indian. He didn't make them alike. He gave the white man a heart to love peace, and the arts of a quiet life. He taught him to live in towns, to build houses, to make books, to learn all things that would make him happy and prosperous in the way of life appointed him. To the Native man the Great Spirit gave a different character. He gave him a love of the woods, of a free life, of hunting and fishing, of making war with his enemies and taking scalps. The white man doesn't live like the Indian - it isn't his nature. Neither does the Indian love to live like the white man - the Great Spirit didn't make him so.\"", "\"Father, - We don't wish to do anything contrary to the will of the Great Spirit. If he had made us with white skins, and characters like the white men, then we would send our children to this school to be taught like the white children.\"", "\"Father, - We think that if the Great Spirit had wished us to be like the whites, he would have made us so. As he hasn't seen fit to do so, we believe he would be displeased with us, to try and make ourselves different from what he thought good.\"", "\"Father, - I have nothing more to say. This is what we think. If we change our minds, we will let you know.\"", "What Day-kau-ray says there shows what the Indians hold to be true: that the arts and the sciences come to the white man because the Great Spirit himself is his teacher in them. The Great Spirit has given the Indian no such lessons. To go after that knowledge by some side road of his own would therefore be a thing beneath him."],
  s32: ["Little incidents of that kind, and now and then a dinner or a tea-party for the young officers — sometimes at the Major's quarters, sometimes at ours — carried our life along pleasantly enough. Now and again I amused myself by making something very nice, a fruit cake or a pie, and sending it over to the young officers' quarters as a present, on the theory that a mess with no lady to preside over it might run short of that sort of thing. Mrs. Twiggs had well-trained servants to do for her what, thanks to my little dark handmaid, always came back around to me.", "One day I made some mince pies. The Major and my husband both approved of them highly, and I thought I would send one to each of the young officers.", "It happened that my husband, coming back that same day from superintending his men on the other side of the river, had an errand that took him to Captain Harney's quarters.", "Dinner had just gone on the table. The Captain would not hear of his leaving, and sat him down with himself and another gentleman who was there. The pork and beans were pronounced excellent. When they were cleared away, a mince pie came on.", "The Captain cut it and helped his guests, then took a piece himself and began to taste it. He pushed his plate back with a jerk and an exclamation, and called to his servant, a little thick-set mulatto who waited on him. \"David, you yellow rascal, how dare you put such a pie on my table?\" Then he turned to the company to apologize. \"If there is anything on earth David does understand, it is how to make a mince pie, and here he has filled this with brandy, so we can't eat a morsel of it!\"", "\"Please, sir,\" said David, modestly, \"I didn't make the pie — it is one Mrs. Kinzie sent as a present.\"", "That left the poor Captain nowhere to stand. He raved at himself in front of them all, and begged my husband in the most earnest terms not to tell me what a mistake he had made — a request that went straight out of my husband's head the moment he was inside his own door. As for the unlucky Captain, he did not venture to call on me again until he was quite sure the whole circumstance had gone out of mine."],
  s33: ["Early in January the snow came down and did not stop. We had an unusual quantity of it at the Portage. In the diggings, as the lead-mining country was called, it lay at a depth nobody had heard of before — five or six feet on the level.", "The officers had sent an express to Chicago to carry our letters down and bring the mail back. The messenger was a soldier named Sulky, a tough, hardy man who had always made light of the distance and of the pack on his shoulders, and who went the whole way on foot. Sulky had now been gone some weeks. We had given him up altogether, and settled it privately among ourselves that he had died somewhere out there of cold and hunger.", "Then, at last, he came in. He was nearly blind from traveling in the snow. He had lain by three weeks in an Indian lodge because the snow lay too deep to let him move a step, and the account he gave of the country between here and there finished the hopes I had been quietly building of getting down to see our friends at Chicago that winter.", "We had been laying plans for it before the last heavy fall. Captain Harney had kindly begun putting together some trains — boxes set on sledges — which lined with buffalo-skins would have made a very comfortable sort of vehicle, and I was still inclined to think that a good deep bed of snow over the whole country was no great obstacle to a sleigh-ride. The commanding officer ended the discussion. He had been violently against the scheme from the first, and he stated plainly that he would order the sentinels to fire on us if we tried to leave the fort. The majority was against us. We gave it up.", "Sweet, lovely little Lizzie Twiggs arrived before January was quite out, and there was light and joy in two houses at once. She seemed to belong to all of us. As she grew in size and beauty it was hard to say which of us was proudest of her. Whatever slow hours we might have had before, we had none now — she was the pet, the darling, the joint property of both households.", "Any regret I had felt about leaving my friend for the three weeks we meant to allow ourselves in Chicago went away with that. She would hardly miss me now. We could hold ourselves ready to move on the first break in the weather and carry out the plan at last.", "Through the last part of February the cold slackened. The snow melted off, and by the beginning of March the weather had turned so warm and genial that we were confident of making the trip on horseback without any serious trouble.", "Once the thing was settled, the first question was warm clothing. A riding-habit of stout broadcloth was pronounced indispensable to my equipment. I did not own one. Nothing in my wedding traveling gear came anywhere near answering the purpose. The cloth itself could be had in abundance at the sutler's store — the shantee, as everyone called it — but that left the question of who was to turn it into a garment.", "The regimental tailor was sent for. He was also cook to one of the companies, and there was some doubt at first whether he could be spared from the spit for the needle as long as I would need him. All his tailoring until then had been done at odd hours on a bench in the company kitchen, and he proposed to carry the riding-habit over there and work on it in the usual way. I pointed out that if I was to superintend the work I should then have to take up my abode in the barracks for the duration, which would be a decided inconvenience.", "He got around the difficulty himself, and was in the end so happy as to find a soldier in Company D who consented to officiate in his place at the kitchen fire until his term of service to me should expire.", "So picture a little solemn-looking man in his stocking-feet, sitting cross-legged on an Indian mat by my parlor window. He had arranged everything to suit himself, and I judged it wisest not to interfere. The cutting-out was the hard part, and since he had never made a lady's riding-habit in his life, the cutting-out fell to me. I was as complete a novice as he was. I will admit that this first effort of mine was open to criticism. The little tailor did not think so. He was in an ecstasy over our joint performance.", "\"Upon my word, madam,\" he would say, standing back to survey it with admiring eyes, \"we shall have a very respectable garment!\" I could not count how many times he said it in the three days the work took.", "I do not believe he had perfect confidence in the culinary powers of his comrade in Company D. Every day, half an hour before the beat of the drum, the work was folded and set aside, the snips gathered up, everything restored to order. Then he would slip out, put on the shoes he had left outside the door in the Turkish manner, and hurry across to the barrack-kitchen to see how matters stood.", "Downstairs, in the meantime, great preparations were going forward under our tidy, active little French servant, Mrs. Pillon, the wife of one of the engagés, who had taken the place of the irregular and unmanageable Louisa.", "Biscuits were baked. A ham, some tongues, and several pieces of salt pork were boiled. Coffee was roasted and ground, sugar cracked, isinglass cut up into pieces of the size wanted for a pot of coffee. Cotton bags of different sizes had been made up beforehand to hold each of these things, and large sacks of skin, which the Canadians call porches, were provided for the bulkier provisions. It was going to be a long trip.", "The distance from Fort Winnebago to Chicago is not formidable if you take the direct route. We knew the direct route was impossible at that season. The road by Kosh-ko-nong was out of the question, since all the Indians were away from their villages in the winter, and with the ice gone there would be no means of getting across the Rock River at that place.", "That left one way only: south to Dixon — Ogie's Ferry, as it was called then — the sole certain crossing of that broad, fast water. The detour ran so far out of our true course that we could not hope to do it in under six days, and we laid in our provisions accordingly.", "While the wardrobe and the food were being got ready, the other arrangements were settled — who would come with us and what we would ride.", "Mr. Kinzie decided to take only two men, Plante and Pierre Roy. Plante was to act as guide, on his own assurance that he knew every mile of the way from the Portage to Ogie's Ferry, and from Ogie's Ferry to Chicago.", "The merits of the different saddle-horses were argued over and the most eligible one picked out for my use. For a while we hesitated between Le Gris and Souris, two much-vaunted animals belonging to Paquette, the interpreter. In the end, being governed like most of my sex by a regard for exterior, I chose Le Gris. Souris was given to young Roy. My own little stumpy pony, Brunet, was pronounced just the thing for a pack-saddle. My husband rode his own bay horse, Tom, and Plante, the gayest and proudest of the party, was mounted on a fine large animal named Jerry that had lately been bought for me. With that our cortége was complete."],
  s34: ["We said our goodbyes, and on the morning of the eighth of March we were mounted and ready. It was a fine day. The streams had already gone green along their edges and were throwing the sun back at us, and everything about that morning promised a kind season, and an early one. At the ferry below the hill the fort stood on, Major Twiggs tried once more, and then again, to talk me out of going. He told me plainly that the journey would be dangerous past anything I was capable of imagining. I would not be moved.", "Every young officer in the garrison had decided he would ride out with us as far as Duck Creek, four miles off, and none of them would hear of being refused. One or two, I think, would gladly have come the whole way, if only to break the long sameness of a life in which nothing whatever occurs. Behind us came an ox cart with a canoe riding on top of it. The canoe was to carry us over the creek, and over a wide marsh beyond it that at this time of year always stood deep in water. We laughed most of the way at the cart, which pitched and banged along a road that had never before in its life been asked to consider a wheeled carriage.", "Our Canadian — a short, shock-headed, sunburnt, thick-lipped man who by some miracle happened to be the husband of my pretty servant, Mrs. Pillon — kept up a running roar at the oxen whenever they slackened or knocked the cart against a stump. \"Marchez, don-g,\" \"regardez,\" \"prenez garde,\" and we found it endlessly funny. My own spirits were high. I could see no hardship anywhere ahead of me and had somehow got it into my head that I was setting out on a pleasure excursion across the prairies. That a straw bonnet and kid gloves might be the wrong things to wear had not once occurred to me. I had never travelled in so raw a season and knew nothing at all about preparing for one; when my husband suggested blanket socks and a woollen capuchon for my head and shoulders, I laughed at him and went without. So when the wind got under my head-gear and shoved it about with a rude puff, and when my hands began to swell and stiffen with the cold, I told myself these were trifles and that I would soon be used to them. I was far too pleased with the novelty of my outfit to mind — the hunting-knife in its gay scabbard hanging at my neck, the tin cup at my saddle-bow.", "At Duck Creek we parted from the young men, who stayed on the bank long enough to watch us over: ourselves in the canoe, the poor horses swimming, the water carrying cakes of ice.", "The far bank rose, and past the rise lay the marsh, perhaps three hundred yards of it. The men hauled the canoe across to its edge. The water there was not deep, so our people only lifted the pack off Brunet and my side-saddle off Le Gris, so that nothing would be lost if anything went wrong, and then got on their own horses and led the two spare ones. My husband set the pack-horse's load and my saddle in the middle of the canoe and took up the paddle.", "\"Now, wifie,\" he said, \"jump in, and seat yourself flat in the bottom of the canoe.\"", "\"Oh, no,\" I said, \"I will sit on the little trunk in the center. I shall be so much more comfortable, and I can balance the canoe exactly.\"", "\"As you please, but I think you will find it isn't the best way.\"", "He shoved off hard and we slid a few feet clear of the bank. The two greyhounds we had brought with us had been standing there whining, refusing the water they had been ordered into. Now they made up their minds. They jumped together and came down square on top of me. For a moment the canoe held. Then it gave, and quick as that, dogs and baggage and lady went into the deepest water there was.", "My husband had been on the point of stepping in himself when the dogs so unceremoniously went ahead of him. He was beside me before I had time to be afraid, and he had me by the collar of my cloak, begging me not to be frightened. I was not, in the least. I was laughing while he lifted me out and set me back on the bank.", "The saddle and the little trunk were fished out after me, thoroughly soaked. And then our amusement was topped altogether by the maladroit Pillon, who was making a hopeless business of riding my spirited Jerry across the marsh. He had both arms around the animal's neck and a face wrung out of shape with terror, and he was shouting every French objurgation he owned. Jerry pranced and curveted, and then pitched his rider — his burden, rather — headforemost, several feet out into the water.", "A general howl of mirth went up at the unfortunate Frenchman, and doubled when he came up out of his watery bed puffing and snorting and waddled back to the place he had started from. The horse, meanwhile, showed the better sense of the two and made his way off to join his companions, who had already reached the farther bank.", "\"Well, wifie,\" said Mr. Kinzie, \"I can't trust you in the canoe again. There is no way but to carry you across the marsh like a pappoose. Will you take a ride on my shoulders?\"", "\"With all my heart, if you will promise to take me safely.\" And I was soon mounted.", "I must confess that the gentleman staggered once or twice under me, for I was no light load, and more than once I was quite sure I was about to go the way of old Pillon. But we reached the other side in safety.", "There he made me change into dry shoes and stockings and — must I admit it? — drink a little brandy against the effects of my icy bath. He wanted to stop altogether, kindle a fire and dry my clothing and my wardrobe properly, and I would not hear of it. I argued that standing still would chill me worse than riding on in a wet habit and cloak, and that may well have been true. But there was something else mixed in with my convictions, which was that I could not bear to give our friends at the fort the pleasure — and they would certainly have taken it — of laughing at so inauspicious a commencement.", "The horses were soon put in order and we started again. It was a fine day for the season. The wet clothes never troubled me at all; the exercise of riding took the chill straight out. The whole manner of travelling was new to me, and I liked it the better for having been shut up more than five months inside the walls of the fort, where the only variation on any day was an occasional walk of half a mile into the surrounding woods.", "We lost a little more time on the road when we met Lapierre, the blacksmith from Sugar Creek, who was going up to the Portage for supplies with one of his associates. So we had come no more than twenty-three miles when we reached the ground we meant to camp on. It lay along a beautiful stream that feeds one of the Four Lakes — that chain whose banks have no equal anywhere for romantic loveliness.", "I could only admire the sagacity of the horses. They seemed to know, the way a person knows, that they were coming to the place where the day's toil would end. Long before we were near the point of woods that meant a halt, their ears went up and their pace quickened, and they came into the spot at a full gallop.", "We got down in an open space just inside the verge of the wood — the timber, as travelers in the West call it. My husband told me to walk about and keep moving until there was a fire, and there was one soon enough; our woodsmen were active and practised, and a large tree came down under them in a very few minutes. The dry grass all around made excellent tinder, and the sparks off the flint took it — there were no loco-focos in those days — and with broken branches and bits of light-wood fed in, we had a cheering flame. Meanwhile the bourgeois put up the tent, facing the fire, but set so that the wind would carry the smoke and flame away from the opening instead of into it. Inside, on the ground, went a bear-skin, then two or three blankets — each rider carried two, one under the saddle and one above it — and once the rest of the luggage had been brought in I could get out of every wet thing I had on and into dry ones. If you want to know what the thermometer was doing, consider my riding-habit. It was laid over the end of the huge log our fire was built against, and within a very few minutes it had frozen so stiff that it stood upright on its own, a dress out of which a lady had vanished by some means nobody could account for.", "To describe the ham broiled on the \"broches,\" the toasted bread, the steaming coffee and the primitive table-furniture would only be to tell the Fox River over again. There is this difference. On horseback we carry a coffee-pot, a tea-kettle, and for each rider a tin cup and a hunting-knife, and nothing else. Ceremony at table does not exist. A man draws his knife out of the scabbard, and if he happens to think of it he gives it a wipe on the napkin. Its first duty is to stir the cup of coffee. Its second is to divide the piece of ham lying on the half of a traveling biscuit held in the left hand, the biscuit doing the office of a plate. To cut the meat cleanly without also destroying the dish beneath it is an art that comes only with long practice.", "We take our places around the mat and eat, and after a ride like that day's we find it delicious. The Frenchmen sit a little distance off with their own supplies of coffee and meat and bread, and now and then pass a joke with the bourgeois, who is a demi-god to them; nothing he does, however affable, however condescending, ever costs him an ounce of their respect or their devotion.", "When the meal is finished, the table-furniture is rinsed out in hot water and set aside until morning. A wisp of dry prairie-grass is generally judged enough to make a knife fit to go back into its scabbard. At this season there is no amusement to be had but watching the spancelled horses shuffle awkwardly from one spot to the next in search of pasturage, so it is not long before we arrange our blankets and lie down to rest.", "At break of day the bourgeois shouts us awake — \"How! how! how!\"", "Everyone is up at once. The fire, fed once or twice through the night, is brought back to a flame. The horses are caught and saddled while a breakfast, the same food as the evening before, is got ready; the tent comes down; the pack-horse is loaded; \"tout démanché,\" as the Canadian says. We eat, rinse the kettles and the cups and tie them to our saddle-bows, and then we are mounted and away, leaving our fire, or rather our smoke, to tell of our visit."],
  s35: ["March 9th. - Our route that day took us by the first of the Four Lakes, and an encampment of Winnebagoes lay scattered along the bank of it. They set up a loud and joyful noise at the sight of their Father - \"Bon-jour, bon-jour, Shaw-nee-aw-kee,\" \"Hee-nee-kar-ray-kay-noo?\" (how do you do?)", "Then came the announcement we could always count upon, \"Wys-kap-rah tshoonsh-koo-nee-noh!\" (I have no bread.)", "That is their way of asking. We were in no position to be generous. If our own stores gave out there was no saying where more would be found, and that uncertainty held us to the strictest economy.", "But the encampment in the morning sun was a beautiful thing. The matted lodges, with blue smoke curling off the tops of them. The trees and the bushes powdered over by the light snow that had come down in the night. The lake almost at our feet, shining, the whole surface of it sparkling. Even the Indians, in their own peculiar costume, belonged to the picture and made it better.", "I was sorry to leave, and we left in haste, because Souris, the pack-horse, had taken our talk with our red friends as his opportunity and decamped. He had at least had the sense to keep to the route in the right direction, so a short race brought us up with him. Young Roy, whose particular charge he was, gave him a great deal of scolding and a few blows besides, and then he was set in the middle of the cavalcade and made to ride there, disgraced, for having broken his duty.", "Beyond the lake our road ran over a \"rolling prairie,\" bare and desolate enough at that season. Snow lay in the hollows, thawed part of the way through, so that a horse could never be certain of his footing, and I could not help laughing along with our Frenchmen, whose laughter rang out, when Brunet and Souris, the two ponies, went floundering nearly to their bodies through the yielding mass. Even Plante, who was vainglorious about his horsemanship and prided himself on it, came once or twice within an inch of being unhorsed, having chosen his road badly. Here and there the rises carried a thicket or a copse, and out of these the dogs would generally start a deer, or more than one. That first bound - the \"lope\" - was the signal, and the chase was on. The horses caught the spirit of the thing as one \"halloo\" answered another, but we never had the good fortune of a shot. Once or twice the dogs came up with what they were after, and had not the strength to hold it. We came up to the Blue Mound somewhere near the middle of the afternoon. I rejoiced to have got even that far. I was fatigued past telling, and by then a single mile was doing the work of two. The miles in this country are unconscionably long. Then I was told that seven of them still lay between us and \"Morrison's,\" where we meant to stop for the night, and I was nearly in despair. It was my first journey on horseback, and I was not yet inured to the exercise.", "When we got to Morrison's my husband reached up to lift me down out of the saddle, and I was so far spent that I fell into his arms.", "\"This will never do,\" he said. \"tomorrow we must turn our faces toward Fort Winnebago again.\"", "Their door stood open to us in the kindest way. Two women received us there — one of them with a face as sweet and benignant as any I have seen, and beside her another, younger by some years. The first of them was Mrs. Morrison. The younger was Miss Elizabeth Dodge, whose father is General Dodge.", "My husband carried me to a small bed that stood in the room where the two had been sitting over their work. They got off my bonnet and my riding-dress, chafed my hands, and made me warm wine and water, and I was soon revived. Half an hour of lying still refreshed me enough that I could talk with the ladies and set my husband's mind at rest on my account. Tea was announced not long afterward, and we crossed to an adjoining building to take it, for Morrison's, like every settler's establishment of that period, was a group of detached log houses, each holding one apartment, or at the most two.", "The table groaned under the good cheer set out on it, and it called up for me certain tables I had once sat at among the old-fashioned Dutch residents along the Hudson.", "My spirits had come back to me and we made a cheerful party. Mrs. Morrison told us that for her first eighteen months in this country not a single white woman had spoken with her; her husband and two black servant-women were the whole of her society.", "A woman from Tennessee had come in just before tea, bringing her little son with her, and the two of them gave us a description to amuse Mr. Kinzie with. Courtesy was the whole of her errand. She was little and dumpy, her complexion burned by the sun to a perfect red, and her hair was an exact tow-color, braided into two tails — one taken up from the front of her forehead, one from the back of her neck — which met on the crown of her head and were held there by a small tin comb. Checkered homespun was her dress, and a \"very tight fit\" it was; there was neither ruff nor handkerchief at her throat, so that she looked like somebody being got ready for execution. Company overawed her, plainly. She was for departing again the moment she had come; but the boy did not intimidate so easily, and he took no meaning at all from her signs or her pinches until he had worked his way over beside Mrs. Morrison and, tugging that old hat of his down farther across his eyes, begged a whang from her — meaning by it a narrow strip of deer-skin. She very obligingly got a large smoked skin out of its receptacle and cut him one, and mother and son took themselves off, smiling, and looking rather scared.", "After tea we went back into Mrs. Morrison's parlor, where nothing would content her but that I take the little bed again — to recruit me, as she put it, against the next day's traveling. My husband, meanwhile, had gone off to see how his men and his horses were to be accommodated.", "In the talk that followed it came out that Mrs. Morrison had passed a good deal of time near my own recent home in Oneida County; that a number of people I had loved and valued there were her friends as well; and that when word reached her of my arrival at Fort Winnebago she had proposed to travel up and visit me, so as to begin an acquaintance which other means, none of them expected, had gone ahead and begun for us.", "We talked on together, long and pleasantly, until the hour had got late, and there was satisfaction on both sides in bringing up old friends and by-gone events for review — all of it edifying to Miss Dodge, and to the gentlemen too, once they had come back and joined us."],
  s36: ["We breakfasted cheerfully the next morning, with the Rev. Mr. Kent, of Galena, at the table with us, and then made ready to go on. My husband had wanted to turn back; I had talked him out of it by telling him, truthfully, that I felt as fresh and as bright as I had the day we left home.", "The one thing anybody seemed uneasy about was whether we would manage to strike the trail to Hamilton's diggings, which was where we were bound next.", "The directions were not what I would call clear. Follow the trail we were on for so many miles; come to a crossing; turn into it and hold easterly; after a while that would put us on a deep trail running straight to Hamilton's. Out on that open country there is nothing to steer by. Every rise of ground is the twin of the last one, and a man who loses his trail out there has about as much chance of picking it up again as of finding a footpath in the middle of the ocean. And a trail is not a road. It is a narrow line cut into the sod by the hoofs of horses that the Indians ride single file, and it sinks so deep into the turf that there are times you cannot make it out at a distance of a few rods.", "None of it was country Mr. Kinzie knew; his journeys from the Portage down to Chicago had always gone the direct way, by Kosh-ko-nong. So he made Mr. Morrison say the directions over, and over again after that, while Plante, who was our guide, swaggered about and talked big, and declared that \"he knew every hill and stream and point of woods from that spot to Chicago.\"", "We were not many miles out before it became plain that Monsieur Plante did not know the country at all. My husband took the lead himself after that and worked his way forward as best he could on the directions he had been given. No crossing trail appeared, none of what we had been promised. The path under us kept bending further from the direction we knew to be right, until at last there was nothing to do but leave it behind.", "We rode the whole of that day and stopped only briefly at noon, to bait the horses and get a little luncheon into ourselves. I cannot describe how desolate that ride was. Rolling prairie without a tree or a watercourse in it. One swell of ground after another, and at the top of each we looked out hoping for a far-off fringe of timber, and got instead the same cheerless country running on without any end to it. The only break in it anywhere was an occasional long, narrow opening cut in the ground, like a giant's grave, where somebody had dug for signs of a lead-mine and found none.", "We wanted the trail back so badly — the cold was deepening, and the wind had gone sharp enough to cut — that we would not be drawn off our course even by the gaunt prairie-wolf that showed itself more than once, watching us over the nearest rise as though it would like us to come and try it. The Frenchmen shouted and put spurs in the instant they saw it, and Kelda and Cora went for it, but the bourgeois called dogs and men back each time, and reminded them that our whole business was to keep moving. Just before sundown we got ourselves with some trouble across a muddy stream that had a thin line of trees along it, enough to make a tolerable camping-place, and we took it gladly, having no idea whether we were near what we were looking for or nowhere near it at all.", "We had covered fifty miles at the least since leaving Morrison's, and yet I felt hardly any fatigue. What I did feel was a low, unsettled discomfort at being lost in that wild, cold country, a thing wholly new to me. But my husband said, in that encouraging way of his, \"Cheer up, wifie - we will find the trail tomorrow,\" and it went away.", "The men soon had the camp in decent order, though the ground was frozen too hard to take a tent-pin easily and there was no timber big enough for the sort of fire we wanted. It was a stony side-hill, which is what they would call it in New England, where they have plenty of them. We were in no position to be fastidious. We ate our salt ham and toasted our bread and listened with pleasure to the Frenchmen talking, who could not say enough about the heroism of \"Madame John\" under the vicissitudes that kept befalling her.", "By bedtime the wind was high enough to be a nuisance, and it kept rising through the night. Then it began to snow hard. We lay there expecting at any minute to have the tent taken off us by the wind. That was not how it went. The poles snapped instead, and the whole of it came down on us at once with all the snow it had been carrying.", "Mr. Kinzie got his men up and took them off into the nearest wood to cut a new set of poles. That left me holding the entire weight of it on my shoulders, because the only shelter I had from the storm was to stay where I was, snug underneath the canvas.", "They contrived some kind of support for the covering at last, and it held well enough until daylight. By then we were so damp and so wretched that we were glad to be up and standing over the fire until breakfast was got through and the horses were saddled again.", "What we saw when we looked out was not encouraging. Snow all round us, unbroken, every direction the same. We had no compass, and the driving sleet thickened the air so that half the time there was no telling where the sun stood in it. I tied my husband's silk pocket-handkerchief over my veil to keep the wind and the flying ice off my face — they cut like a razor — and even shielded every way that could be managed out there, I suffered from the cold more than I had known was possible.", "On we went, mile after mile, turning into every point of woods we came to on the chance of finding an Indian wigwam where we might at least ask a question. Every one of them was empty. Deserted, and with no sign that anybody had lit a fire there lately to encourage us that a living soul was within miles of where we stood.", "Then the man at the head of the party shouted, and every heart in the line jumped.", "\"Une clôture! une clôture!\" (A fence! a fence!)", "It was almost like life coming back into the dead.", "We spurred forward, and there, sure enough, were a few straggling rails along the crown of a rise not far ahead of us.", "And at that moment a cock crowed somewhere beyond it, and there has never been music in this world half so sweet."],
  s37: ["We followed the line of the inclosure down the far slope and found a cluster of log cabins at the bottom of it — low, shabby, nothing about them to raise anybody's hopes. They were also shelter, and the storm was coming down hard on us.", "\"Whose cabins are these?\" Mr. Kinzie asked a man who was splitting wood at one of the doors.", "\"Hamilton's,\" the man said, and he came straight over to help us down, hospitality being a thing nobody in that wild country stops to consider.", "We were put into the best-looking of the buildings. A big fire was going in the clay chimney and the room held a genial warmth, though there were gaps of several inches beside the doors and the windows. A woman got up from a seat beside a sort of bread-trough that was doing duty as a cradle for a fine, fat baby. She wore a tidy calico dress and a shabby black silk cap trimmed with lace shabbier than the cap. She made us room at the fire. She did not offer to take my wrappings and defences off me, heavy as they now were with snow — whether she was too timid for it or simply did not know, I could not tell.", "I contrived, with my husband's help, to get free of them, and when he had seen me comfortably settled and in a fair way to thaw out after that freezing ride, he left me and went to see after his men and his horses.", "He was gone a long while. I assumed he would bring our host back with him; instead he came back alone, and laughing. Mr. Hamilton, it seemed, could not bring himself to appear before me. He did not want somebody who had known his family in the East to see the life he was living now. Whatever the feeling was, it passed, because he came in before dinner and was introduced to me, and he was every bit as agreeable and as polite as a son of Alexander Hamilton naturally would be.", "The housekeeper — she was married to one of the miners — got us a dinner, plain and comfortable, and a table was laid out as long as the cabin's dimensions would allow. The end of it nearest the fire had the nicer furniture on it and the more delicate fare. The rest of it did not.", "A horn was blown. That brought in miners, ten or twelve of them, and they took the places below ours. I have never in my life seen a rougher-looking set of men, and they spoke as uncouthly as they looked. Deer-skin, all of it — hunting-shirts, trowsers, moccasins — the shirts fringed along the seams with more of the same, and every man wearing a colored belt at the waist with a large hunting-knife stuck through it. Each of them had the look of a brigand.", "They called Mr. Hamilton, so far their superior as he was, \"Uncle Billy\" — all of them, uniformly. I could not help fancying there was something desperate in them that had to be propitiated by that kind of familiarity. What one of them said afterward did nothing to argue me out of it. He hung back when the rest of the gang had taken themselves off. He had heard we came from Fort Winnebago, and he told us \"he was a discharged soldier, and would like to make some inquiries about his old station and comrades,\" and then sat himself down without being asked and started putting questions to us.", "He spoke of his former officers with a bitterness that settled it for me: the man was a deserter. I rather suspected, too, that he had run from the service to escape a punishment. His face twisted out of shape when he came to Captain H., whose company he had belonged to. \"There is a man in the mines,\" he said, \"who has been in his hands, and if he ever gets a chance to come within shot of him, I guess the captain will remember it. He knows well enough he darsn't set his foot in the diggings. And there's T. isn't much better. Everybody thought it a great pity that fellow's gun snapped when he so nearly had him at Green Bay.\"", "Having got all of that off his chest, he marched out, and I was more relieved than I can well express.", "Mr. Hamilton spent most of the afternoon with us. The storm went on so violently outside that travelling any farther was out of the question. He told us a great many pleasant anecdotes and reminiscences — his early life in New York, and what had befallen him since he came out into the Western wilderness — and when business obliged him to leave us for a while he furnished us with books to pass the time. The most interesting of them was the biography of his father.", "If that illustrious man could have foreseen the scene in which his book would one day be perused — the home of his own son — what would his sensations have been?", "The most amusing part of it was still ahead of us. All through the late afternoon I had been turning over the question of where we were to be put for the night. Our landlady, pale and melancholy, and her fat baby were plainly the only specimens of the feminine gender anywhere about that establishment, so there was little reason to hope that any of the other cabins held the wherewithal for a comfortable lodging; and this one, so far as the eye could tell, offered nothing of the sort but two beds standing against the farther wall, neither of them curtained. In time the question answered itself. I watched the hostess run a cord between the two beds and hang petticoats and spare garments along it by way of a partition. Then she invited us to take one of them.", "My whole preparation was to pull the cloak round me and lie down facing the wall. The good people were less ceremonious. At a distance of barely two feet there was no mistaking the sound their clothes made — not \"laid aside,\" but sent flying over the partition-wall that stood between us.", "And what we thought about, while we were still awake to think anything at all, was only how thankful we were for so comfortable a lodging after everything the day had put us through. We did not think it long. Our eyes closed and we slept."],
  s38: ["Morning came up clear and bright. Sleep had put us back together, and we looked forward to taking up the journey again with real pleasure, certain that the rest of it would give us no more trouble. Mr. Hamilton offered to ride with us as far as his next neighbor's — twenty-five miles, a distance he waved off as trifling. Beyond that, from Kellogg's on to Ogie's Ferry on the Rock River, the track was traveled enough that we could not lose it. That was his assurance.", "One of the miners came to see us off — the one who owned the wife and the baby, and who was, for that reason, a shade more human than the men he worked beside. His farewell was that he wished us well out of the country, and that we might never have occasion to return to it.", "\"I pity a body,\" he said, \"when I see them making such an awful mistake as to come out this way, for comfort never touched this Western country.\"", "Mr. Hamilton was as good a companion that day as he had been the day before, and a perfectly desperate rider. He set a pace that would have left me somewhere behind if I had still been on my pony. I had the fine, noble Jerry under me now, and I needed him.", "We were all well mounted and he still nearly rode away from us. The prairie had given out. We were among the branches of the Pickatonick, in country that had gone rough and broken, and we went down into ravines and up out of them and through passes so narrow that the branches met overhead. They were interwoven and they projected, and as I came through behind the others I paid them my veil in pieces. Once they caught my hat, and I would have hung there like Absalom if my husband had not sprung to me — Jerry being far too keen on holding his place in the race to do anything but what the mule did under the unfortunate prince.", "Nobody called a halt. We rode at that same rate until three o'clock in the afternoon, so that when we came in at last to Kellogg's there was no arguing the distance: we had made thirty miles at least. What wore on me hardest through all of it was not the pace but the little beast Brunet. He had been a saddle-horse until now, and he had been used to a place well up in the file, near the guide, near the front. Being demoted to pack-horse and put at the back did not suit him. Every time we came down to a stream the file would break apart, each rider choosing his own place to ford, and every time — every single time — the moment I turned Jerry's head into the water, Brunet came past me at a rush and flung himself into the exact spot where we meant to go. He plunged and snorted and threw the water over me, and more than once he startled Jerry sideways and very nearly put me into the stream. I learned after the first two crossings to hang back and let him have it his own way. That changed nothing. What he wanted was not to go first; it was to go in at the same instant Jerry did. In the end I struck a bargain with young Roy, who got down at every stream and held him until I was safely on the other bank.", "Kellogg's was a comfortable mansion set just inside the verge of a pleasant grove of timber, which is what a Western traveler calls a small forest. Mrs. Kellogg proved to be a very respectable-looking matron, and she let us know early that she came from the city of New York. Mr. Hamilton's presence at her table plainly gratified her, and she found occasion to tell us why: in earlier days she had done needle-work for his family.", "She gave us an excellent dinner. Afterward she settled me into a rocking-chair beside a large fire and put the \"Life of Mrs. Fletcher\" into my hands to keep me occupied, while the gentlemen went out to look over the place, and Mr. Kellogg's stock, and then, more carefully, their own animals. We had meant to push on to Dixon's before dark. The snow started again, and that settled it.", "When it came out that we were bound for Chicago, Mr. Kellogg made up his mind on the spot to come with us; he had business to do there, he said. Mrs. Kellogg set about getting him ready to leave in the morning. I argued against it. The next day was Sunday, and I do not travel on Sunday, and I pleaded for one more day where we were. I lost. There was the necessity of the case, and the weather that could not be relied on, and the plain importance of getting through this wild country as fast as we were able. All I carried was a delay in the hour of starting — late enough in the afternoon to leave us just time for the sixteen miles to Dixon's before the sun went.", "Mr. Kellogg needed almost no time to prepare. He would take two days' provisions and no more. At his brother-in-law Dixon's we would have our supper and our breakfast, and from Dixon's to Chicago was a day and a half; he knew that road well.", "By that arithmetic what remained in our own packs was enough to see us to the end of the journey. My husband asked Mrs. Kellogg to bake us another bag of biscuits anyway, against accidents, and he suggested to Mr. Kellogg that he might do well to carry something beyond his limited allowance. The good man would not have it. He was unwilling to burden his horse any further than was absolutely necessary, and at this season, he pointed out, the animals had to carry their own fodder on top of everything else. It will be seen that we had reason to rejoice in our own foresight.", "After the night before, my standards had come down considerably. So when I was shown our sleeping-apartment and understood that we would be sharing it with six men, travelers like ourselves, the only thing I felt was gratitude that every bed in the room had a full suit of blue checked curtains, which made a very tolerable substitute for a dressing-room."],
  s39: ["It was late in the day on the thirteenth of March before we got away from our kind hostess. She sent us off loaded with cakes, with good wishes, and with messages for her sister Dixon and for the children. The riding was pleasant. The country had winter all over it and was beautiful anyway.", "There was a house at Buffalo Grove where we halted for half an hour, and a nice-looking young girl there made us a present of maple-sugar she had boiled herself. She had a story to tell us as well, about the rivalry between two claimants for the patronage of the stage-wagon. The proprietors had not yet settled whether to run it through Buffalo Grove or by another road, which she pointed out to us a short distance off. The driver, she was careful to let us know, was for Buffalo Grove. We asked her, \"Is he a young man?\" She said he was, and the blush that came with the answer explained the whole matter to our satisfaction.", "The sun was just going down when we came to the Rock River, dark and running fast. We had gone a long way out of our road for the sake of that ferry. The ferry turned out to be a skiff. The larger boat had been torn loose the week before and carried off downstream when the ice broke up.", "My husband thought first about getting me over. He put me into the boat with the saddles and the packs and the rest of it, and since it was so late and there was no time to spare, he took the bridles of the two most docile horses in the same hand, to guide them while they swam.", "A few rods out from shore we heard a hard puffing and blowing close by us and turned to see what it was. It was little Brunet, come up on our weather-bow. He had decided, entirely on his own responsibility, that he would not be outdone by Jerry, whom he took for his model, and he made the opposite shore as soon as anybody in the party.", "Everyone landed safely, and a short walk brought us to Mr. Dixon's house. He had not been long in that country, but he had already got everything about him comfortable, and when he showed us into Mrs. Dixon's sitting-room and sat us down by a glowing wood fire, and Mrs. Dixon went to work on a nice supper for us, I decided the comfort more than paid for the inconvenience of such a trip.", "There were several children around her. One of them stood leaning against the chimney-piece in the full Indian costume — calico shirt, blanket, leggings. He had a dark complexion and full, melancholy eyes, and he kept them down on the ashes, where he was making marks with a stick. He hardly lifted them to look at us at all, which is the one thing children always do. He interested me exceedingly. There was a bright little girl close by, plainly a daughter of our host, and I asked her, \"Who is that boy?\"", "\"Oh, that is John Ogie,\" answered she.", "\"What is the matter with him? he looks very sad.\"", "\"Oh, he is fretting after his mother.\"", "\"Is she dead, then?\"", "\"Some say she is dead, and some say she is gone away. I guess she is dead, and buried up in one of those graves yonder\" — and she pointed out the window at two or three little picketed inclosures on the rising ground opposite.", "My sympathy for the child was already strong, and it grew when I asked the little spokeswoman, \"Has he no father?\" and she answered, \"Oh, yes, but he goes away, and drinks, and don't care for his children.\"", "\"And what becomes of John then?\"", "\"He stays here with us, and we teach him to read, and he learns dreadful fast.\"", "Then the boy raised those large dark eyes and turned them on me, and it went to my heart. It was such a motherless look. What was behind it I only understood long afterward, when I learned the rest of his history. His mother was living. He knew she was living. He never said so to the other children — his people keep such things behind them. She had married a surly, intemperate Canadian, and when she could no longer endure the way he used her she left him and went back to her own family among the Pottowattamies. Years later this boy and a brother, who had also been left behind with their father, found their way to the Upper Missouri to join her, the Government having by then taken her and the rest of her tribe away from the shores of Lake Michigan.", "Supper came smoking to the board, ducks and venison and everything that belongs with them, and we did it ample justice. Traveling is a great sharpener of the appetite, and so is cheerfulness, and ours rose considerably while Mr. Dixon gave us his encouraging account of the road still ahead of us.", "\"There is no difficulty,\" he said, \"if you keep a little to the north, and strike the great Sauk trail. If you get too far to the south, you will come upon the Winnebago Swamp, and, once in that, there is no telling when you will ever get out again. As for the distance, it is nothing at all to speak of. Two young men came out here from Chicago, on foot, last fall. They got here the evening of the second day, and, even with a lady in your party, you could go on horseback in less time than that. The only thing is to be sure and get on the great track that the Sauks have made, in going every year from the Mississippi to Canada, to receive their presents from the British Indian Agent.\""],
  s40: ["The next morning came up bright and lovely, which for that season of the year was a gift, and we said our good-byes to Mr. and Mrs. Dixon in high spirits. For the first few miles our way lay along the banks of the Rock River, which rise and fall in long easy swells, and we held always eastward, on the beaten path — road, rather — that runs down to Fort Clark, or Peoria. The Sauk trail, we had been told, cut across this road about six miles out.", "When we had covered, by our own reckoning, the whole of that distance, a trail opened in front of us bearing northeast, and we halted to argue whether this was the one we wanted.", "My husband thought not. It leaned too far north for his liking, and it was faint — far fainter than any path could be that was trodden every year by so great a body of Indians on their journeys.", "Plante had no doubt whatever. This was the exact place where he and \"Piché\" had come out upon the great road the year before, on their way to Port Winnebago. \"On that very rising-ground at the point of woods, he remembered perfectly well stopping to shoot ducks, which they ate for their supper.\"", "Mr. Kellogg would commit himself to nothing. He agreed with whoever had spoken last.", "Plante was \"the guide,\" and so sure of himself that it was settled we should follow him — though not without grumbling from the bourgeois, who kept calling a halt to reopen the whole question.", "\"Now, Plante,\" he would say, \"I am sure you are leading us too far north. Why, man, if we keep on in this direction, following the course of the river, we shall bring up at Kosh-ko-nong, instead of Chicago.\"", "\"Ah! mon bourgeois,\" the Canadian would answer, light as ever, \"would I tell you this is the road if I weren't quite certain? Only one year ago I traveled it, and can I forget so soon? Oh, no - I remember every foot of it.\"", "What convinced Monsieur Plante at last was the water swinging away ahead of us in the river's great bend, and the bold rocky bluffs standing over it.", "\"Are you satisfied now, Plante?\" my husband asked him. \"By your leave, I will now play pilot myself.\" He quit the trail there and set our course as nearly due east as he could hold it.", "The weather had turned by then, and turned hard; the cold was intense. Even supposing we were right at last, the days we had thrown away were no small matter. We had taken on nothing more in the way of provisions at Dixon's, choosing to load what forage we could carry for the horses instead, since the picking around our camping-grounds was thin and never made them a proper meal. Still, the belief that we had found the right path at last carried us along, and we rode until dark, when we made a comfortable \"encampment\" at the edge of a grove beside a little stream.", "I have never known cold like that night. Salted provisions were what I was used to, and they left me with a thirst past bearing, so my husband had got into the way of setting the little tin coffee-pot full of water at the head of my bed when we lay down. That night it was a solid block of ice long before midnight. Inside the tent, wrapped up as we were in blankets, we took no harm at all. Outside the tent the air was pitiless.", "March 15th. — The bourgeois had us up and packing at the first show of light. The Sauk trail had to be found today, whatever it cost us. And if it were not found — what then? No one asked it aloud. No one had an answer to give.", "A little way from camp the ground softened under the horses and we were into marsh country. Wild geese, brant and ducks went up out of it screaming in their thousands, and the farther lakes and ponds lay black with them. The night's cold had frozen the shallow water we were trying to cross, but not enough: the ice would not carry a horse, and it cut their feet and ankles at every step as they broke through. Now and then we could not get forward at all and had to turn back and work our way round the head of the marsh — so that on top of everything else we carried the knowledge that we were laboring all day long and going nearly nowhere.", "We were clear of that swampy country at last, and onto firmer footing, mostly open prairie. But the trouble only changed its shape. The weather moderated, and with it came a snow-storm that blinded us. We had no trail we could trust and no compass; the sun was the one guide left to us, and now the air had thickened until not a man among us could have said which quarter of the heavens he stood in.", "We kept moving, and our track must have wandered badly. Many miles on we crossed an Indian trail cut deep into the ground, running square across the line we had been holding. The snow gave out, the cloud thinned enough to show us where the sun was, and we saw that this new trail ran north and south. Now what? Hold to the east, or take the path, on the chance it would carry us into the road we had spent so many days hunting? And if we took it, which way — up or down? My husband wanted south. By his reckoning we were still too far north, somewhere about the Grand Marais, or Kish-wau-kee. Mr. Kellogg and Plante both wanted north, Plante insisting that his bourgeois had overshot to the south already, that we must by now be somewhere near the Illinois River. Outvoted, my husband gave way, and we turned the horses' heads north with his whole will against it. A few miles of that was all he could stand. \"You may go north, if you please,\" he said, \"but I am convinced that the other course is right, and I shall face about - follow who will.\"", "So we came about and rode south, and the miles were long and heavy, and the only thing that broke them was poor Mr. Kellogg's fretting. \"I am really afraid we are wrong, Mr. Kinzie. I feel pretty sure that the young man is right. It looks most natural to me that we should take a northerly course, and not be stretching away so far to the south.\"", "My husband gave no sign of hearing any of it. The Frenchmen said nothing. They would have parted with their right hands sooner than set themselves against the bourgeois once he had spoken his decision, and it would never have entered their heads to volunteer an opinion or a remark unless they were asked for one.", "For mile after mile the road ran on through prairie, and then it bent round a point of woods and put us, without any warning, at the edge of an Indian village. The whole party gave a shout. Nothing answered it. We galloped up among the wigwams and not a voice called back to us, not a dog barked. The place was as still as a grave. We rode round it and round it, then got down and put our heads into several of the big huts. They had stood empty a long while. Bare bark walls, and nothing else — every article of furniture stripped out and carried off by the owners to their wintering-grounds, to be brought back in the spring when they came again to lay out their corn-fields and take up their summer cabins.", "What that did to us I will not try to set down. We got back into the saddle heavy-hearted and went on, and the snow began again, and made a bad situation worse. In time we halted for the night. We had known for some while that our provisions would not stretch to another day, and here we were — no one could say where — with woods and prairie all round us, a long way from any human home, and food enough for one thin supper.", "The dogs came whining about us for their share, as they always did, and got a bare bone between them. We lay down that night knowing that if we were not hungry yet, we would be hungry by morning.", "Morning came. Plante and Roy had a good fire going and a nice pot of coffee ready, and that was breakfast, for when the bag was shaken out and turned inside out it gave up three crackers and nothing more, and the others would not hear of my leaving them; I was to put them in my pocket for my dinner. Mr. Kellogg touched me very much then. He went into his wallet and brought out a piece of tongue and a slice of fruit-cake, which he said \"he had been saving for the lady since the day before, for he saw how matters were a going.\"", "Poor man. He would have done better to take my husband's advice at the start and lay in a larger store for himself. What he had brought ran out early on the second day, and for the last two meals he had been boarding with us."],
  s41: ["The trail was still under us, and we held to it until about nine o'clock. Then the wood opened out and there was a river in front of us, broad and running fast, with a cluster of Indian wigwams standing on the far bank. The trail went straight on down into the water, which said plainly enough that the stream could be forded. We never got the chance to prove it. The break-up of the week before had stacked ice along the banks, tier upon tier, and we searched up and down for some place to bring ourselves down to the water and could not find one.", "The men shouted, and shouted again, on the chance that some straggler from the village might be within hearing with his canoe. Nothing came back to us but the echo of our own shouting. What were we to do? I looked at my husband. He was still speaking cheerfully, but the care had settled into his face. \"We will follow this cross-trail down the bank of the river,\" he said. \"There must be Indians wintering near, in some of these points of wood.\"", "I will admit that our prospects dismayed me. I kept up a show of courage all the same and let nobody see how low I had sunk. The whole party was dull and gloomy already.", "So we went on along the bank, which stood well up above the water, with a thick wood set back a little from it. Then my horse, who held a mortal terror of Indians, started to prance and jump beneath me, snorting, ears pricked, certain that an enemy stood somewhere close. I screamed up the line to my husband, who rode at the head of it, and what I felt was pure delight. \"Oh, John! John! there are Indians near - look at Jerry!\"", "At that instant a little Indian dog came out from under the bushes at the roadside and set up a barking at us. No sound was ever more welcome to me. We rode straight into the thicket, dropped down into a small hollow, and found two squaws crouched behind the brush, doing their best to keep themselves out of our sight.", "The relief came over them when Mr. Kinzie spoke to them in the Pottowattamie language. \"What are you doing here?\"", "\"Digging Indian potatoes\" - (a species of artichoke.)", "\"Where is your lodge?\"", "\"On the other side of the river.\"", "\"Good - then you have a canoe here. Can you take us across?\"", "\"Yes - the canoe is very small.\"", "They brought us down the bank to the water's edge where the canoe was lying. It was very small indeed. My husband explained to them that they would have to carry me across first and then come back for the rest of the party.", "\"Will you trust yourself alone over the river?\" asked he. \"You see that but one can cross at a time.\"", "\"Oh, yes\" - and in a moment I was laid flat in the bottom of the canoe with nothing above me but the sky. The older of the two took the paddle and knelt at my head; the younger, a girl of fourteen or fifteen, knelt down at my feet. There was exactly room for me to lie between them that way, the two of them at the opposite ends, and no room to spare.", "While all this was being got ready, Mr. Kinzie was asking the women where we had come to. They had no name for the river except \"Saumanong,\" which tells nothing, being the word they use for any large stream. But he worked out of them that the village we had passed higher up, on the other side of the water, was Wau-ban-see's, and with that he knew where we stood: on the Fox River, and probably some fifty miles from Chicago.", "To all his questions the squaw gave the same assurance, that Chicago was \"close by.\"", "\"That means,\" he said, \"that it isn't so far off as Canada. We must not be too sanguine.\"", "The men began stripping the packs off the horses, and while they worked I was paddled over. The old woman turned around and went straight back for the next load, leaving the girl behind with me for company. I found the trunk of a fallen tree and sat down on it in the snow and looked out across the dark water. I am not ashamed to own the weakness. For the first time in the whole journey, I cried. It was not hunger that brought it, nor fear, nor the cold. It was the plain desolation of spirit, the sickness of heart that comes of \"hope deferred,\" and of all the evils I know of, that one is the hardest to bear.", "The poor little squaw stood looking into my face, wondering at me and sorry for me at once. I suppose she was working out privately what a woman mounted on so fine a horse, in a broadcloth dress as comfortable as mine, could possibly find to cry about. I pointed to the place on the log beside me, but she would rather stand and go on watching me with that same pitying look. A young companion came out and joined her, and after a short burst of chattering in which I was plainly the subject, the two of them trotted off into the woods and left me alone with my own thoughts.", "\"What would my friends at the East think,\" I said to myself, \"if they could see me now? What would poor old Mrs. Welsh say? She who warned me that if I came away so far to the West, I should break my heart?", "Would she not rejoice to find how likely her prediction was to be fulfilled?\"", "That was enough to rouse me. I dried my face, and by the time my husband and his party and every horse and every article of luggage were over the water, I had my cheerfulness back again and was ready for whatever came next."],
  s42: ["The old squaw led and we went after her, and her lodge stood only a little way back among the trees. I had never once been inside an Indian lodge. I had looked into them — there are always a cluster of them standing around the Interpreter's house at the Portage, and I had peeped in at those — but I had never gone through a door.", "Inside, everything had its place. Four sticks of wood laid in a square in the middle of the floor served for a hearth, and the fire burned inside that square, and the smoke went up and out through the opening at the top. The mats that made the walls were new and neatly done. Bags of their own manufacture hung against the sides from the poles of the frame, holding dried food and whatever else the family counted as treasure, and from the cross-poles hung ladles and small kettles and wooden bowls. In the center, swinging from an iron chain, a large kettle hung over a thin fire, with something dark and suspicious-looking seething in it. Mats were spread on the floor between the fire and the outer wall, and my husband told me to sit down there and make myself comfortable.", "When an Indian meets a white person the first thing asked for is bread. They are exceedingly fond of it. So I was not surprised, after my husband had gone out of the lodge, when the woman opened our talk with a timid \"pe-qua-zhe-gun choh-kay-go.\" I had enough Pottowattamie in me to know what it meant. I have no bread.", "I shook my head. I tried to make her understand that giving was beyond me, that I had not so much as had my own breakfast. She got my meaning at once. She took down a bowl and ladled it full of Indian potatoes out of the kettle on the fire and put it in front of me. I was in no condition to be fastidious. My appetite had a good deal to do with it, I am sure — but I thought them delicious.", "Two little girls belonged to the lodge, and they sat and stared at me with an admiration and an astonishment that only grew when I took my little Prayer book out of my pocket and began to read. They had certainly never seen a book in their lives. What amused me was their manners: they kept their faces carefully turned away from me while they asked their mother what I could possibly be doing, and listened to what she answered.", "I was reading when a sound came from the doorway — a sudden \"hogh!\" — and the mat that hung across the entrance lifted and a man came in with that springing, graceful bound that belongs only to them. The master of the lodge. He had been out after ducks and was just back. He was tall and finely made, with an open, cheerful face, and while his wife told him quietly who I was he was taking off his accoutrements, in the most unembarrassed and well-bred manner it is possible to imagine.", "My husband came in not long after. He had been seeing to the horses, and helping the men get their fire going and their tent up, which the rising wind was making no easy work.", "From the Indian he had learned where we were. This was the Big Woods, or Piché's Grove, called so for a Frenchman of that name who lived not far off. The river we had crossed was the Fox River. He would guide us to Piché's, and from there the road ran perfectly plain, or he would take us the whole way into Chicago if we liked that better — but his advice was that we stay camped where we were for the day, because there was a storm coming. In the meantime he would go and shoot ducks for our dinner and our supper. We gave him powder and shot, and he went straight back out after the game.", "I had a roll of scarlet ribbon in my pocket. I had put it there when we left home, thinking a stout string might be wanted for something. I took it out now and cut two yards for each of the little girls with the knife that hung at my neck. They were delighted. Their mother took each length and divided it in half and tied a piece to each of the small clubs their hair was knotted into at the temples. Then the two of them looked at each other and laughed and cried \"Saum!\" and their mother laughed with them — though she seemed to me to be holding some of it back, not quite willing to show a stranger how proud she was of them.", "When the tent was in order my husband came to fetch me, and we said our thanks to our friends in the wigwam and went out.", "The storm had arrived. The trees around us were bending and cracking, and the whole air was full of wild-fowl going south ahead of the blast, screaming and quacking as they came. Our tent stood among the trees a short way from the water. My husband walked me down to the bank so that I could see, for one minute, what we had gotten out of. The wind came down out of the north in a perfect hurricane. The river ran loaded with masses of snow and ice, dancing along on the torrent, and above the torrent went thousands of birds, and the noise they made set the whole woods ringing.", "One hour later and the crossing would have been impossible. There would have been nothing left for us but to stay where we were and starve in the wilderness. I do not know how any of us could be grateful enough to the kind Providence that had carried us through such dangers.", "The men had felled an immense tree and built the fire up against the trunk of it. But the wind would not settle. Every five minutes it swung, and the tent filled with smoke, and I had to get out into the open just to breathe. Then I would go and sit on one end of that huge log, as close to the flames as I could manage, because the cold was dismal — and the wind seemed to act on nothing but caprice, since whichever side I chose was the side the smoke and the hot ashes came at, puffing straight into my face until I was half blind. My veil was no defense. Neither was a silk handkerchief. I was thankful when our huntsmen came back with a great many ducks, because plucking them and getting them ready with the men gave me something to think about besides myself.", "We borrowed a kettle from our Indian friends. It was not remarkably clean, so we warmed a little water in it and scoured it out with prairie hay before the birds went in. With that and a bowl of Indian potatoes, which our kind neighbors made us a present of, we had an excellent soup before long.", "Between the cold and the smoke and the ashes and cinders driving at me all afternoon, it was the most uncomfortable stretch of the journey so far, and I was glad when the dark came and I could creep into the tent and pull the blankets over me and be out of reach of all three at once.", "In the night the storm came on ten times worse. Trees were going down all around the camp, and every crash brought us up out of whatever sleep we had, because there was no telling which one was coming down on us. Tired as we were, we hardly slept at all. It was the morning that showed us properly what had been happening in the dark. Within sight of the tent, fifty trees at least — giants, every one of them — lay flat on the ground.", "We ate our scanty breakfast and got mounted, and then had to pick our way out foot by foot, the road so blocked with fallen trunks that there was barely a line through them."],
  s43: ["Our Indian guide came to us early and took us carefully out of the timber. As we went he pointed out bee-trees, one after another; that grove was famous for them, he said. Then he set off at a long trot and held it, and a little after nine he brought us out at Piché's — a log cabin on a rise, its face turned east over the broad prairie. We had counted on food and drink there. Some of the party knew Piché of old. The door opened on a room full of people, and not one of them was him. Travelers, a few. Indians, a great many.", "Nothing there tempted us to stay except the fire, a bright one burning in the clay chimney, and the chance to warm ourselves at it. A man in Quaker dress came forward to answer our questions. He was bound for Chicago himself, he said, and would take us there. So we thanked our Indian friend and paid him well for all the trouble he had so kindly taken on our account, and he went back the way he had come.", "From Piché's the prairie ran unbroken to the Du Page. Between the two forks of that river, our new acquaintance — Mr. Dogherty — told us we would find the house of a Mr. Hawley, and at it a comfortable dinner.", "The cold that day was past anything I have words for. The wind came over that open ground with nothing standing in its way, and it went through to the heart of every one of us. When my feet went numb I beat them against the saddle to bring the blood back, and I kept beating until they were too bruised to beat any longer. Nothing offered itself. Not a house, not a wigwam, not so much as a clump of trees to put at our backs, mile after weary mile. Then the west fork of the Du Page, frozen — but not hard enough to bear the horses. There was nothing for it but to cut them a road. It took time. The ice had thickened to several inches in that one bitter night. Plante went ahead with the axe and cut as far as his arms would reach, then mounted one of the hardy little ponies and broke the ice in front of him until he had opened a passage clear to the opposite shore.", "The horses shook as they were reined in among the floating pieces, and it was a pitiful thing to watch. Those of us waiting on the bank in that wind were in no position to pity anybody. Brunet must have arrived at the same conclusion, because he did what Brunet always did and went in directly behind Plante, out of his turn, and stood quaking at his heels, turning his head now and again to look back at the rest of us, as much as to say, I've got ahead of you, this time. We were all over at last. We put the spurs to them and did not slow until we came up to Hawley's — a large, roomy house near the east fork of the river.", "The good woman of the place took us in and soon had us warm and comfortable, and it was like stepping back into a settled country. She began on dinner at once. We watched every motion of it, eager-eyed. She reached a huge ham down from the rafters and cut innumerable slices off it; she broke a dozen and more fine fresh eggs into a pan, ready for frying; she mixed a johnny-cake and propped it against a board in front of the fire to bake. With that bright fire going the business still took an unconscionable while. It was done at last, and she might well stare at the travelers' appetites we had carried in with us. She had no way of knowing what short commons we had been on for two days.", "We asked, and learned that if we pushed on we could make Lawton's, on the Aux Plaines, that same night — and that would put us within twelve miles of Chicago. We did not waste a minute we did not have to waste. We were in the saddle again as soon after dinner as it could be managed.", "The east fork was worse than the west had been. Something had already broken the ice — the current, or riders ahead of us who had cut their own way through — and it was coming down in large cakes. The horses had to make a rush of it. The water was high and running in a torrent, and if I had not been up on Jerry, the tallest horse in the cavalcade, I would have come out of it splashed terribly.", "As it was I was well frightened, and I took hold of bridle and mane together and did not let go of either. After that we traveled as fast as the ground allowed, because we meant to reach our destination before dark.", "Mr. Dogherty, a tall man, bolt upright, half Quaker and half Methodist, spent those miles doing his best to entertain me. He laid out a thorough schedule of his religious opinions, together with the Scripture on which each of them rested. He was a good deal of a perfectionist, and it was plain that he regarded himself with no small satisfaction as a living illustration of his favorite doctrine.", "\"St. John says,\" — this was the style of it — \"St. John says, 'He that is born of God, doth not commit sin.' Now, if I am born of God, I don't commit sin.\"", "I was too cold and too weary to take up the point, so I let him have the whole of it his own way. I believe he must have thought me rather a dull companion. He gave me the credit, at least, of being a good listener."],
  s44: ["The light was nearly gone by the time Lawton's came in sight. The Aux Plaines[20] had frozen over, and the house stood on the far bank with the ice between. We shouted until somebody came out of the building. He set to work cutting a channel and working a canoe through it toward us, and it took so long that by the time he reached us the shapes on the shore had blurred together.", "It was worth the wait. Lawton's turned out to be a thoroughly comfortable house — carpets on the floor, a stove going, everything quite in civilized style. Mr. Weeks, the man who had brought us across the water, was running the place as major-domo while Mr. Lawton was away.", "Mrs. Lawton was young, and not ill-looking. She was also bitterly unhappy, and she said so: the loneliness of her condition, and the fact of her having been \"brought out there into the woods, which was a thing she hadn't expected, when she came from the East.\" We did not ask her what she had expected of a wild and unsettled country. We only said what people say — that in a few years things would be better. She answered that \"She didn't mean to wait for that. She should go back to her family in the East, if Mr. Lawton didn't invite some of her young friends to come and stay with her, and make it agreeable.\""],
  s45: ["It was hard to take in, when we got up the next morning, that twelve miles of prairie were the whole of what lay between us and Chicago le Désiré — which is what I could not stop myself from calling it.", "We could see clear across the plain, and at the very edge of it stood two tall trees. My husband told me he had put them in the ground himself, when he was a boy. They had grown high enough by now to serve as landmarks, and they stayed in front of us the whole length of the beaten road. I said the same thing over and over to myself as we went: \"There live the friends I am so longing to see! There will end all our trials and hardships!\"", "A Mr. Wentworth fell in with us on the road, and we asked him for news of the family; it had been a long time since any word of them had reached us. Once we were into Chicago he brought us to a small tavern at the forks of the river. That part of the place was called Wolf Point in those days, after an Indian who had lived there named \" Moaway,\" or \"the Wolf.\"", "\"Dear me,\" the old landlady of the little tavern said to us, \"what dreadful cold weather you must have had to travel in! Why, two days ago the river was all open here, and now it's frozen hard enough for folks to cross a-horseback!\"", "My husband did not care to trust it, whatever she said. He decided to leave the horses where they stood and go the rest of the way on foot — about half a mile — to the house where his mother and his sister lived.", "So we walked, over the ice first and then along the northern bank of the river. Genevieve caught sight of us — she was a half-breed servant of the family — and she did not stop to greet us. She turned and flew into the house, calling out, - \"Oh! Madame Kinzie, who do you think has come? Monsieur John and Madame John, all the way from Fort Winnebago on foot!\"", "A moment after that we were being held by those dear, kind friends of ours. Somebody was sent off to \"the garrison\" to bring in the rest of the family, and for that one day, at any rate, I was the wonder and the admiration of the entire circle, \"for the dangers I had seen.\""],
  s46: ["The buildings of Fort Dearborn then were the buildings that stand there now. Better kept in those days, though a good deal of it was already run-down even then. They went up in 1816 with Captain Hezekiah Bradley overseeing the work, and there was a tale in circulation about him: that his regard for the Government's purse ran so high he made the soldiers whittle wooden pins to fasten the timbers rather than spend spikes and nails on them, and that he called the junior officers out to build alongside the enlisted men, whose work it properly was. If any of that is true, the captain was laboring under a delusion — a forgivable one in a man who had spent his life on the frontier — that the Government thanks its servants for an excess of economical zeal.", "High pickets closed the place in, with bastions set at every other angle. Big gates faced north and south, and small posterns were cut here and there for the convenience of the people inside. The strip of river bank running west — the light-house buildings sit on it now, and docks close it in — held the garrison's root-houses. South of the pickets lay the parade-ground, and past that the company gardens, thick with currant-bushes and young fruit-trees.", "You would take the fort to be standing at the river's mouth. It was not. The river in those days made a turn, ran around the promontory the fort was built on, bent south, and met the lake about half a mile lower down. The fort was on the right bank. The left was a long spit of land reaching out from the northern shore and forming part of it. In 1833 the United States Engineers, sent to build a harbor at that point, cut through that portion of the left bank and threw the piers out, and after that the water came over the whole tongue of land. It has been eating at the southern bank ever since and has taken many valuable acres off it, while the same motion of that vast body of water lays new ground down, constantly, on the north of the harbor.", "Between the gardens and the river bank stood the house of Jean Baptiste Beaubien. Farther south there was a rickety tenement that Mr. John Dean, the sutler of the post, had put up many years before. Not long after Chicago began to grow, the lake worked in under its foundations until the whole thing went over backward down the bank, and there it lay for a long while afterward, a melancholy spectacle.", "Straight across the water from the fort, on the north bank, was my husband's family mansion. It was long and low, a range of four or five rooms, with a piazza running the length of its front. A broad green space lay inclosed between it and the river, shaded by a row of Lombardy poplars. Behind the house stood two immense cottonwood-trees; one of them is standing yet, an ancient landmark. North of the house was a fine garden, well cultivated, and around it all the buildings the establishment required — dairy, bake-house, a lodging-house for the Frenchmen, and the stables.", "Between the house and the lake ran a vast range of sand-hills, grown over with stunted cedars, pines and dwarf willows. The lake at that time was not more than thirty rods off.", "Going on from there up the northern bank you came first to the Agency House. Cobweb Castle, it had been named during the long stretch when a bachelor lived in it, and the sobriquet stuck to it ever after. It stood where the southwest corner of Wolcott and N. Water Streets is now. Plenty of people still remember it: a substantial, compact little building of hewed and squared logs, a center with two wings, and — to be strictly accurate — two tails as well, because once the sides had no room left for additions, the additions went on behind, wherever a vacant spot could be found.", "From the front none of that showed, and the whole thing looked symmetrical enough. Then the town improved itself, and a street was opened, maliciously, directly across the rear of the building, and after that the establishment with all its comical little adjuncts was a standing entertainment for anyone who passed by. No matter. A great many of Chicago's early settlers can testify to the pleasant, happy hours passed under that odd-shaped roof.", "Log buildings stood grouped around the Agency House, quarters for the various men the Government employed there — blacksmith, striker, laborers. Canadians and half-breeds, most of them, with now and then a stray Yankee to set everything going by his activity and enterprise.", "One more house stood on the north side of the river. A former resident named Miller had built it and then gone off to \"Rivière du Chemin,\" or Trail Creek, which about that time people were beginning to call \"Michigan City.\" His house, near the forks of the river, stood empty.", "On the southern bank there was nothing at all between the fort and \"The Point,\" which is what the forks of the river were called then. That ground was low wet prairie, hardly fit to walk on in the driest part of the summer and flatly impassable the rest of the year. A muddy little stream — a slew, in the language of this country — came winding around from about where the Tremont House stands now and fell into the river at the foot of State Street. At the Point, on the south side, Mark Beaubien had just finished a house: two stories, white, pretentious, with bright-blue wooden shutters, and the whole little circle at Wolf Point admired it. He kept a canoe ferry there to carry people across the south branch of the river.", "Facing down the river from the west, the first thing was a small tavern kept by Mr. Wentworth, whom everyone knew as \"Old Geese\" — no reflection on his shrewdness, only a compliment to one of his own cant expressions. Close to him were two or three log cabins occupied by Robinson, the Pottowattamie chief, and some of his wife's connections. Billy Caldwell, the Sau-ga-nash, stayed here at times with his wife, who was a daughter of Nee-scot-nee-meg, one of the most famous chiefs of the nation. A little way off from these stood a small square log building meant originally for a school-house, though it served as a place of worship whenever some itinerant minister turned up.", "The Clybourns had settled before this near where they live now, on the North Branch; they called their place New Virginia. Four miles up the South Branch stood an old building that had once drawn a great deal of interest, having been the theater of some stirring events in the troubles of 1812. It went by Lee's Place, or Hardscrabble. A settler named Heacock was living in it then.", "The roads being bad the greater part of the year, anyone going between the fort and the Point generally went by water — a boat rowed up the river, or a canoe under some skilful hand. The same canoes kept up the intercourse between the people at the fort and those at the Agency House.", "Two companies of soldiers held the garrison. Of the officers, Lieutenant Furman had died the autumn previous, and several more were away on furlough. Major Fowle and Captain Scott both being absent, the command devolved on Lieutenant Hunter. There were Lieutenants Engle and Foster besides him; Foster was unmarried. Dr. Finley, the post surgeon, was gone as well, and Dr. Harmon, a gentleman from Vermont, was supplying his place.", "My husband's mother, his two sisters and his brother were living at the Agency House; the family residence near the lake had J.N. Bailey, the postmaster, in it.", "The Dean House held a Mr. and Mrs. Forbes, who kept a school. Gholson Kercheval ran a small trading establishment out of one of the log buildings at Wolf Point, and John S.C. Hogan had charge of the sutler's store in the garrison.", "There was also a Mr. See, lately come into the country, living at the Point, who would hold forth in the little school-house of a Sunday — less to the edification of the people listening than to the unmerciful slaughter of the \"King's English.\"", "That, I believe, is every white inhabitant of Chicago at a date less than half a century back. To a good many who read these pages the foregoing particulars will surely seem uninteresting. But for anyone who comes to see Chicago, and still more for anyone who comes to make it their home, there may be something in looking back at its first beginnings, in considering the almost magical change a handful of years has wrought, and in reading out of that past the marvelous prosperity still ahead of it."],
  s47: ["Where the name Chicago came from is still argued over. Some of the Indians trace it to the fitch, the polecat; others to the wild onion that once grew thick all through these woods. On one point nobody disagrees: the place took its name from an old chief who drowned in the stream in former times. Tradition has kept that death carefully, but it must belong to a very remote period, and there is a paper to prove it — an old French manuscript that General Cass carried back from France.", "It offers itself as a letter. M. de Ligney writes from Green Bay; M. de Siette, off among the Illinois, is the man he writes to; and the date on it is as early as 1726. The place is set down there as \"Chica-goux.\" That same spelling turns up again in family letters from the opening years of the present century.", "When the Indians give the early history of Chicago, they give it with great simplicity: \"the first white man who settled here was a negro.\"", "His name was Jean Baptiste Point-au-Sable. St. Domingo was where he came from, and about the year 1796 he made his way into this remote region and began a life among the Indians. Between those two races there is usually a strong affection. Jean Baptiste imposed on his new friends by letting them believe that among the whites he had been a \"great chief.\" Perhaps it galled him that the Pottowattamies never raised him to any such dignity; at any rate he left the vicinity, and he ended his days in Peoria, sheltered by a friend named Glamorgan — another man out of St. Domingo, of his own color, who had come into large Spanish grants at St. Louis and in the country round it, and who for a time had an extensive landed estate to his name.", "Point-au-Sable had made some improvements at Chicago, and those a Frenchman named Le Mai took possession of, and began trading with the Indians there. The place did not stay his either. A few years on, John Kinzie, Esq., bought Le Mai's establishment out. Mr. Kinzie was then living at Bertrand — Parc aux Vaches, they called it in those days — near Niles, in Michigan. Except for the military, that gentleman was the only white inhabitant of Northern Illinois for nearly twenty years, so a few particulars of his early years may be worth having."],
  s48: ["He was born at Quebec in 1163. His mother had been married before, to a gentleman by the name of Haliburton, and that marriage produced one daughter — the woman who would become the mother of General Fleming, of Nicholas Low, Esq., and of Mrs. Charles King, of New York. She is remembered as a lady of remarkable beauty and accomplishments. Of the second marriage there was one child only, and that was Mr. Kinzie. He was still an infant when his father died. His mother married a third time, a Mr. Forsyth, and the family afterward moved to the city of New York.", "At ten or eleven he was sent to school at Williamsburg, on Long Island, along with two of his half-brothers. Every Saturday a negro servant came out from the city to collect the children and carry them home until Monday morning. One Saturday the man arrived to find the school in an uproar. Johnny Kinzie was gone. They hunted in every direction, turned out every corner of the place, and it came to nothing. The boy was not there to be found.", "The news had to be carried home to his mother. Some said he had drowned. Others said he had only wandered off and would come back on his own. Weeks went by, and then months, and at last he was given up and mourned as a dead child. All that while he was carrying out a plan he had been forming for a long time: to get to Quebec, the city he had been born in, and make his own way in the world.", "However he managed it, he crossed from Williamsburg over to New York, and at one of the docks on the North River he found a sloop bound up to Albany and took passage in her. Somewhere on the river a gentleman noticed him — a small boy traveling alone — and grew curious enough to ask him what his business was.", "\"He was going to Quebec, where he had some friends.\"", "\"Had he the means to carry him there?\"", "\"Not much, but he thought he could get along.\"", "As it happened, the gentleman was bound for Quebec himself. He took the boy in charge, paid his way the entire distance, and finally left him standing in the streets of the city — a city where, whatever he had told himself, he knew nobody at all.", "He walked about for some time, putting his head into one \"store\" and workshop after another. At last he stepped into a silversmith's shop, looked at the master's face, liked what he saw written in it, and asked whether he wanted an apprentice.", "\"What, you, my little fellow! What can you do?\"", "\"Anything you can teach me.\"", "\"Well, we will make a trial and see.\"", "The trial went well enough. He lived with that kind man's family better than three years, until his parents — who, in moving to Detroit, had necessarily come back into Canada — found out where he was, and he was given back to them.", "There were five younger half-brothers, all Forsyths. In the old family Bible there is an entry set down after the family had moved to Detroit, and it reads: \"George Forsyth was lost in the woods 6th August, 1775, when Henry Hays and Mark Stirling ran away and left him. The remains of George Forsyth were found by an Indian the 2d of October, 1776, close by the Prairie Ronde.\"", "That the same unhappy mother should be made to bear that particular grief twice seems a strange cruelty — a child gone in a way that is worse than a death, because it leaves the mind free to supply every horror it can invent. This is how the little brother was lost. He was walking home from school one evening when he met the colored servant-boy riding out to the common to bring in the cows. The school-house stood close by the old fort, and everything west of it was rough open country, uncultivated, known as \"the Common.\" The child asked to be taken up for a ride. The servant said no and told him to go straight home. Two other boys, a little older, were with him, and the three of them trailed after the rider a good way, still hoping to be let up. When the dark came on, the two older boys gave it up and went back. The youngest went on. The servant did not see him again on his return, and no word of him ever came to anyone, though the whole small settlement searched and kept searching — not until the next year, when, as the record says, an Indian found his remains. Nothing about them could be identified except the auburn curls of his hair and the little boots he had worn. He cannot have lived long after he lost his way; the Prairie Ronde lay too close to the settlement for him to have missed the calls and the sounding horns of the men hunting for him, if he had still been alive to hear.", "As he grew, Mr. Kinzie's restless and adventurous turn of mind kept him out on the frontier. He went into the Indian trade young, and kept establishments at Sandusky and at Maumee. Around 1800 he pushed still farther west, to St. Joseph's, in Michigan. That same year he married Mrs. McKillip, the widow of a British officer, and in 1804 he came to Chicago to make his home. That was the year the first fort was built there, by Major John Whistler.", "Little by little he opened trading-posts farther and farther out, every one of them feeding the parent post at Chicago: at Milwaukie among the Menomonees; at Rock River among the Winnebagoes and the Pottowattamies; on the Illinois River and the Kankakee among the Pottowattamies of the Prairies; and among the Kickapoos in the country they called \"Le Large\" — that wide stretch of district afterward erected into Sangamon County.", "Every post had a superintendent over it, and its full number of engagés, its string of pack-horses, its boats and canoes. From nearly all of the stations the furs and peltries came in to Chicago on the backs of the pack-horses, and the trade goods went back out to them the same way.", "Two or three vessels a year, rarely more, came in spring and fall with the supplies and the goods for the trade, and carried away the furs already gathered, taking them to Mackinac, which was the depôt for the Southwest and the American Fur Companies. In the other seasons the furs went the same distance by boat, coasting the shore of the lake."],
  s49: ["The Canadian voyageurs — the engagés — are very nearly gone from the country now, and some notice of them belongs here.", "No other class of men resembled them. Whatever it is that makes a poet, they had the equivalent of it for this work, and they came into the world already fitted for it. They were tough, they were tireless, they were clever with their hands, and they were cheerful past all reason. Set any emergency in front of them and their minds bent around it. Difficulty did not baffle them. Hardship did not put them off. And they loved easily and hard: they formed attachments of the warmest kind to their bourgeois, the master who hired them, and to the native people among whom their engagements carried them.", "Montreal was the depôt — Marrialle, in their own mouths. That was where the agents went when they had a quota to fill for one of the companies or for some independent trader, and that was where they picked over the material and made their selections.", "The wages ran from four to six hundred livres a year, in the old Quebec currency. The rations were one quart of lyed corn and two ounces of tallow a day, or, as the paper had it, \"its equivalent in whatever sort of food is to be found in the Indian country.\" Men have been known to live a whole winter on fresh fish and maple-sugar, every other supply cut off from them, and to do it cheerfully.", "There was a saying about them: \"Keep an engagé to his corn and tallow, he will serve you well - give him pork and bread, and he soon gets beyond your management.\" A bargain, to them, meant exactly what its words said and nothing else, which is how the crew of the old trader M. Berthelet came to grief. He signed them at Montreal on the terms that they should eat when their bourgeois ate, and eat what he ate. He meant it as a piece of fun. The simple Canadians took it for a rare stroke of luck, and for such luxurious prospects as those they counted themselves fortunate men. Berthelet, before they started, had stuffed his pockets with crackers. Now and then, when he was quite certain nobody had an eye on him, he slipped one out and ate it. The pipes went round, and went round again. The men grew hungry. They watched for some sign that a meal was being got ready for the bourgeois, and no such sign came, and since he was fasting they held their tongues and fasted with him.", "Then it stopped being a joke and turned serious, and they could not carry it any further. They asked, in some distress, to be let out of the bargain, and settled gladly for the ordinary rations in place of the dainties they had been promising themselves.", "At Mackinac, which was where the whole fur trade came through, a man drew a small portion of his wages in advance to buy his winter outfit — his pipes and tobacco, his needles and thread, some lengths of bright-colored ribbon, red and yellow gartering, which is quality binding. Those were the small coin he would trade to the Indians for whatever little things he wanted. If he was bound for Lake Superior, or for some post so far north that such articles were not to be had at all, he added one or two smoked deer-skins to make moccasins from.", "Fitted out that way, he entered his three years. Labor through the day; and then, when evening handed him back to himself, the laughing and the joking and the singing and the long stories.", "Here and there you found a man who held to the exact letter of his agreement about what kind of work he had hired on to do. As a rule, though, an engagé stood ready for whatever his bourgeois asked of him, and was as faithful in it as any vassal ever was to his chief.", "They tell a story about M. St. Jean, who traded on the Upper Mississippi. He gave one of his Frenchmen an order: go out with the others, into the forest, and cut wood. The man refused. \"He wasn't hired,\" he said, \"to chop wood.\"", "\"Ah! for what, then, were you hired?\"", "\"To steer a boat.\"", "\"Very well, steer a boat, then, since you prefer it.\"", "It was mid-winter. They walked the objector down to the river and set him in the stern of the boat, which lay where it was, held fast in the ice.", "Two hours of the employment he had contracted for, with the thermometer below zero, and he was perfectly content to go and take his place among the choppers. He never afterward thought it wise to choose his own work.", "There is an aristocracy inside the service, and it is a comical thing to watch. The engagement generally runs three years. A first-year man is a mangeur-de-lard, a pork-eater, and an hivernant — one who has a winter in the country behind him — looks down on him with a contempt that has something sovereign in it. He will not associate with him. If the pork-eater invites him to take a friendly glass, he will manufacture some excuse and decline it. The most inveterate drunkard among them will be tortured by wanting that glass and still never let himself be coaxed across the line. The custom holds.", "Once his first winter is behind him the mangeur-de-lard rises out of the freshman class, and takes his own turn at lording it over whoever comes next.", "They have another habit as well: taking the name of a bourgeois and working it into some absurdity that sounds nearly like it. Kinzie came back from one man as Quinze nez, fifteen noses, and from another as Singé, monkeyfied. Mr. Kercheval was Mons. Court-cheval, short horse. The Judge of Probate was le Juge Trop-bête, too foolish. Here is a case that shows how far it can be carried.", "Mr. Shaw, an agent of the Northwest Fur Company, had spent a great many years on the frontier, and to the voyageurs he was Monsieur Le Chat. On quitting the Indian country he married a Canadian lady, and children came — several of them. His old foreman, Louis la Liberté, arrived in Montreal to pass a winter there some years after his return to Canada, and word of the marriage had reached him, and nothing would do but that he see his old bourgeois again.", "He got his chance in the Champ de Mars, where Mr. Shaw was walking with two officers. La Liberté spotted him, ran straight over, caught him by both hands, and greeted him — \"Ah! mon cher Monsieur le Chat: comment vous portez-vous?\" (My dear Mr. Cat, how do you do?)", "\"Très-bien, Louizon.\"", "\"Et comment se porte Madame la Chatte?\" (How is the mother cat?)", "\"Bien, bien, Louizon, elle est très-bien\" (She is very well.)", "\"Et tous les petits Chatons?\" (And all the kittens?)", "That was further than Mr. Shaw could go. He said shortly that the kittens were all well, turned away with his military friends, and left poor Louizon standing there thoroughly astonished at how abruptly it had ended.", "That was the shape of their isolation — everything beyond them shut away, the garrison their only society — and inside it the family of Mr. Kinzie lived contentedly enough, with every comfort about them and nearly all the luxuries besides.", "The friendship they gave the Indians came back to them, and what it grew into was no ordinary attachment. Eight years passed from Mr. Kinzie's first coming among them. Then 1812 came, and showed exactly what that attachment was.", "The Narrative that follows records some of the chief things that happened in that year."],
};
